-- V478: Seed 124 Class 12 Chemistry Mock Tests (104 Real-Format 70M Papers + 20 Chapter-Wise 25M Mocks)

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_class_id UUID;
BEGIN
    SELECT id INTO v_class_id FROM learn_exam_types WHERE slug IN ('class-12', 'cbse-class-12') LIMIT 1;
    IF v_class_id IS NULL THEN
        SELECT id INTO v_class_id FROM learn_exam_types WHERE name ILIKE '%Class 12%' LIMIT 1;
    END IF;

    IF v_class_id IS NOT NULL THEN
        DELETE FROM learn_test_questions WHERE mock_test_id IN (SELECT id FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id::text LIKE '12020000-%');
        DELETE FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id::text LIKE '12020000-%';

        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

Reason (R): 0.9% (w/v) NaCl is isotonic with human blood plasma osmotic pressure.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 1, 'Equal osmotic pressure prevents net osmosis.', NULL, NULL, 'Equal osmotic pressure prevents net osmosis.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000007', 'State Henry''s Law and explain why scuba divers suffer "bends" (avoided using Helium-diluted tanks).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 2, 'p = K_H x. High underwater pressure dissolves excess N₂ in blood; rapid ascent drops pressure releasing painful/fatal N₂ bubbles ("bends"), prevented by 11.7% He + 56.2% N₂ + 32.1% O₂ tanks.', 'Law (1M) + Bends & He reason (1M)', NULL, 'p = K_H x. High underwater pressure dissolves excess N₂ in blood; rapid ascent drops pressure releasing painful/fatal N₂ bubbles ("bends"), prevented by 11.7% He + 56.2% N₂ + 32.1% O₂ tanks.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000008', 'Distinguish between Minimum Boiling Azeotrope and Maximum Boiling Azeotrope with one example each.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 2, 'Minimum boiling: large positive deviation (95% ethanol + 5% water); Maximum boiling: large negative deviation (68% HNO₃ + 32% water).', '1M each', NULL, 'Minimum boiling: large positive deviation (95% ethanol + 5% water); Maximum boiling: large negative deviation (68% HNO₃ + 32% water).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000009', '45 g of ethylene glycol (C₂H₆O₂, M = 62 g/mol) is mixed with 600 g of water. Calculate (a) freezing point depression and (b) freezing point of the solution (K_f = 1.86 K kg/mol).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 3, 'm = (45/62)/0.6 = 1.21 m. ΔT_f = 1.86 × 1.21 = 2.25 K. Freezing point = 273.15 - 2.25 = 270.90 K (-2.25°C).', 'Molality (1M) + ΔT_f 2.25 K & 270.9 K (2M)', NULL, 'm = (45/62)/0.6 = 1.21 m. ΔT_f = 1.86 × 1.21 = 2.25 K. Freezing point = 273.15 - 2.25 = 270.90 K (-2.25°C).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a', 'Calculate the mass of ascorbic acid (Vitamin C, C₆H₈O₆ = 176 g/mol) to be dissolved in 75 g of acetic acid to lower its melting point by 1.5°C. (K_f = 3.9 K kg/mol)', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 3, 'w₂ = (ΔT_f × M₂ × w₁) / (1000 × K_f) = (1.5 × 176 × 75) / (1000 × 3.9) = 5.08 g.', 'Formula (1M) + 5.08 g (2M)', NULL, 'w₂ = (ΔT_f × M₂ × w₁) / (1000 × K_f) = (1.5 × 176 × 75) / (1000 × 3.9) = 5.08 g.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000b', 'Define Van''t Hoff factor (i) and explain abnormal molar mass due to association (dimerisation of benzoic acid in benzene) and dissociation. Calculate the freezing point of an aqueous solution containing 2 g of benzoic acid (M = 122 g/mol) in 25 g of benzene (K_f = 4.9 K kg/mol), given experimental ΔT_f = 1.62 K — find % association of acid into dimers.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 5, 'i definitions & abnormal M (2M). M_obs = (4.9 × 2 × 1000)/(1.62 × 25) = 241.98 g/mol -> i = 122/241.98 = 0.504. For 2HA -> (HA)₂, α = (1 - i)/(1 - 1/2) = 2(1 - 0.504) = 0.992 = 99.2% associated (3M).', 'Theory (2M) + 99.2% dimerisation (3M)', NULL, 'i definitions & abnormal M (2M). M_obs = (4.9 × 2 × 1000)/(1.62 × 25) = 241.98 g/mol -> i = 122/241.98 = 0.504. For 2HA -> (HA)₂, α = (1 - i)/(1 - 1/2) = 2(1 - 0.504) = 0.992 = 99.2% associated (3M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000c', '(i) Define Reverse Osmosis and name a common semipermeable membrane used in desalination. (ii) Which aqueous 0.1 M solution has highest boiling point: Urea, NaCl, or Na₂SO₄? (iii) Why is glycol added to car radiators in freezing cold regions? (iv) Calculate osmotic pressure of 5% (w/v) urea (M=60) at 27°C (R = 0.0821).', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 4, '(i) Applying P > Π on saltwater forces pure water through cellulose acetate SPM. (ii) 0.1 M Na₂SO₄ (highest i = 3). (iii) Acts as antifreeze lowering freezing point of water. (iv) Π = (50/60) × 0.0821 × 300 = 20.52 atm.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Coastal desalination plants pump seawater across semipermeable cellulose acetate membranes under high hydrostatic pressure to extract potable water cleanly.', '(i) Applying P > Π on saltwater forces pure water through cellulose acetate SPM. (ii) 0.1 M Na₂SO₄ (highest i = 3). (iii) Acts as antifreeze lowering freezing point of water. (iv) Π = (50/60) × 0.0821 × 300 = 20.52 atm.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d', 'With dilution of both strong and weak electrolytes, Molar Conductivity (Λ_m) ALWAYS:', '{"A":"Decreases","B":"Increases (due to reduced inter-ionic attraction in strong, and rising α in weak)","C":"Remains constant","D":"Drops to zero"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 1, 'Conductivity κ decreases on dilution, whereas Λ_m increases.', NULL, NULL, 'Conductivity κ decreases on dilution, whereas Λ_m increases.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000e', 'Charge required to reduce 1 mole of MnO₄⁻ to Mn²⁺ in acidic medium is:', '{"A":"1 Faraday","B":"3 Faradays","C":"5 Faradays (5 × 96500 C)","D":"7 Faradays"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 1, 'Mn(+7) -> Mn(+2) needs 5 electrons = 5 F.', NULL, NULL, 'Mn(+7) -> Mn(+2) needs 5 electrons = 5 F.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000f', 'Kohlrausch''s Law of Independent Migration of Ions helps calculate Λ°_m for:', '{"A":"Only NaCl","B":"Weak electrolytes like CH₃COOH where extrapolation to zero concentration fails","C":"Molten salts only","D":"Non-electrolytes"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 1, 'Λ°(CH₃COOH) = Λ°(CH₃COONa) + Λ°(HCl) - Λ°(NaCl).', NULL, NULL, 'Λ°(CH₃COOH) = Λ°(CH₃COONa) + Λ°(HCl) - Λ°(NaCl).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000010', 'During discharging of a Lead Storage Battery, the cathode (PbO₂) and anode (Pb) both get converted into:', '{"A":"PbO","B":"PbSO₄ (and H₂SO₄ density drops)","C":"PbCl₂","D":"Pb(OH)₂"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 1, 'Pb + PbO₂ + 2H₂SO₄ -> 2PbSO₄ + 2H₂O.', NULL, NULL, 'Pb + PbO₂ + 2H₂SO₄ -> 2PbSO₄ + 2H₂O.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000011', 'Which cell converts chemical combustion energy of H₂ and O₂ directly into electricity with ~70% efficiency and zero pollution?', '{"A":"Dry Leclanche cell","B":"Mercury button cell","C":"H₂–O₂ Fuel Cell (used in Apollo space programme)","D":"Ni-Cd cell"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 1, 'Produces clean potable water by-product.', NULL, NULL, 'Produces clean potable water by-product.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000012', 'Assertion (A): In a Mercury cell (used in hearing aids/watches), cell voltage remains constant at ~1.35 V throughout its working life.
Reason (R): The overall cell reaction Zn(Hg) + HgO -> ZnO + Hg does not involve any ion in solution whose concentration changes during use.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 1, 'Solid/liquid pure activities keep potential constant.', NULL, NULL, 'Solid/liquid pure activities keep potential constant.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000013', 'State Faraday''s First and Second Laws of Electrolysis.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 2, 'First: mass deposited m = ZIt. Second: when same quantity of electricity passes through different electrolytes, masses deposited are proportional to their chemical equivalent weights (W₁/E₁ = W₂/E₂).', '1M each', NULL, 'First: mass deposited m = ZIt. Second: when same quantity of electricity passes through different electrolytes, masses deposited are proportional to their chemical equivalent weights (W₁/E₁ = W₂/E₂).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000014', 'Explain why alternating current (AC) rather than direct current (DC) is used to measure conductivity of an ionic solution.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 2, 'DC causes electrolysis and polarisation changing solution concentration around electrodes; AC prevents net chemical composition change.', 'Avoids polarisation/electrolysis (2M)', NULL, 'DC causes electrolysis and polarisation changing solution concentration around electrodes; AC prevents net chemical composition change.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000015', 'Write Nernst equation and calculate EMF of the cell at 298 K:
Mg(s) | Mg²⁺ (0.001 M) || Cu²⁺ (0.0001 M) | Cu(s)
Given E°(Mg²⁺/Mg) = -2.37 V, E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 3, 'E°_cell = 0.34 - (-2.37) = 2.71 V. E_cell = 2.71 - (0.0591/2) log(0.001/0.0001) = 2.71 - 0.02955(1) = 2.68 V.', 'E° 2.71V (1M) + Nernst & 2.68V (2M)', NULL, 'E°_cell = 0.34 - (-2.37) = 2.71 V. E_cell = 2.71 - (0.0591/2) log(0.001/0.0001) = 2.71 - 0.02955(1) = 2.68 V.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000016', 'Conductivity of 0.001028 mol L⁻¹ acetic acid is 4.95 × 10⁻⁵ S cm⁻¹. Calculate its dissociation constant K_a if Λ°_m for acetic acid is 390.5 S cm² mol⁻¹.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 3, 'Λ_m = (4.95×10⁻⁵ × 1000)/0.001028 = 48.15 S cm² mol⁻¹. α = 48.15/390.5 = 0.1233. K_a = cα²/(1-α) = 0.001028×(0.1233)² / 0.8767 = 1.78 × 10⁻⁵.', 'Λ_m (1M) + α (1M) + K_a 1.78×10⁻⁵ (1M)', NULL, 'Λ_m = (4.95×10⁻⁵ × 1000)/0.001028 = 48.15 S cm² mol⁻¹. α = 48.15/390.5 = 0.1233. K_a = cα²/(1-α) = 0.001028×(0.1233)² / 0.8767 = 1.78 × 10⁻⁵.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000017', 'State Kohlrausch''s Law. Derive the relation between Δ_rG°, E°_cell, and Equilibrium Constant K_c. Calculate Δ_rG° and log K_c for Daniell cell Zn + Cu²⁺ -> Zn²⁺ + Cu (E°_cell = 1.1 V, F = 96500 C/mol). Also explain the electrochemical mechanism of Rusting of Iron with anode/cathode reactions.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 5, 'Kohlrausch (1M). ΔG° = -nFE°_cell = -2×96500×1.1 = -212.3 kJ/mol; log K_c = nE°/0.0591 = 2.2/0.0591 = 37.22 (2M). Rusting: Fe -> Fe²⁺+2e⁻ (anode), O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ -> 2H₂O (cathode), oxidised further to hydrated Fe₂O₃·xH₂O (2M).', 'Kohlrausch (1M) + ΔG° & K_c (2M) + Rusting (2M)', NULL, 'Kohlrausch (1M). ΔG° = -nFE°_cell = -2×96500×1.1 = -212.3 kJ/mol; log K_c = nE°/0.0591 = 2.2/0.0591 = 37.22 (2M). Rusting: Fe -> Fe²⁺+2e⁻ (anode), O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ -> 2H₂O (cathode), oxidised further to hydrated Fe₂O₃·xH₂O (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000018', '(i) Products of electrolysis of aqueous NaCl using Pt electrodes vs molten NaCl. (ii) Time in seconds to deposit 2 g silver from AgNO₃ using 2 A current (Ag=108 g/mol). (iii) Anode & cathode reactions in H₂–O₂ Fuel Cell using concentrated KOH. (iv) Contrast Primary vs Secondary batteries.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 4, '(i) Aq NaCl -> H₂ (cathode), Cl₂ (anode), NaOH remains; Molten NaCl -> Na (cathode), Cl₂ (anode). (ii) t = (2 × 96500)/(108 × 2) = 893.5 s (~14.9 min). (iii) Anode: 2H₂ + 4OH⁻ -> 4H₂O + 4e⁻; Cathode: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ -> 4OH⁻. (iv) Primary non-rechargeable (dry cell); Secondary rechargeable (lead storage, Li-ion).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Electroplating baths and fuel-cell electric buses run continuously by balancing electrode potentials, ion mobilities, and Faraday current efficiency.', '(i) Aq NaCl -> H₂ (cathode), Cl₂ (anode), NaOH remains; Molten NaCl -> Na (cathode), Cl₂ (anode). (ii) t = (2 × 96500)/(108 × 2) = 893.5 s (~14.9 min). (iii) Anode: 2H₂ + 4OH⁻ -> 4H₂O + 4e⁻; Cathode: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ -> 4OH⁻. (iv) Primary non-rechargeable (dry cell); Secondary rechargeable (lead storage, Li-ion).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000019', 'Unit of rate constant k for a Zero-order vs First-order reaction respectively are:', '{"A":"s⁻¹ and mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹","B":"mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ and s⁻¹","C":"L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ and s⁻¹","D":"s⁻¹ and L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 1, 'Zero-order k = [conc]/time; First-order k = time⁻¹.', NULL, NULL, 'Zero-order k = [conc]/time; First-order k = time⁻¹.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001a', 'Half-life t₁/₂ of a First-order reaction is:', '{"A":"Directly proportional to initial concentration [R]₀","B":"Independent of initial concentration [R]₀ (t₁/₂ = 0.693 / k)","C":"Inversely proportional to [R]₀","D":"Proportional to [R]₀²"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 1, 'Zero-order t₁/₂ ∝ [R]₀; First-order t₁/₂ = 0.693/k.', NULL, NULL, 'Zero-order t₁/₂ ∝ [R]₀; First-order t₁/₂ = 0.693/k.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001b', 'Inversion of cane sugar (excess water) and acid hydrolysis of ethyl acetate are examples of:', '{"A":"Zero-order reactions","B":"Pseudo first-order reactions","C":"Second-order reactions","D":"Negative-order reactions"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 1, 'Excess H₂O stays nearly constant so bimolecular reaction behaves as 1st order.', NULL, NULL, 'Excess H₂O stays nearly constant so bimolecular reaction behaves as 1st order.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c', 'In Arrhenius equation k = A e^(-E_a/RT), slope of ln k vs 1/T graph equals:', '{"A":"-E_a / (2.303 R)","B":"-E_a / R","C":"E_a / R","D":"ln A"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 1, 'ln k = ln A - (E_a/R)(1/T) -> slope = -E_a/R.', NULL, NULL, 'ln k = ln A - (E_a/R)(1/T) -> slope = -E_a/R.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001d', 'A catalyst increases the rate of a chemical reaction by:', '{"A":"Increasing ΔH of reaction","B":"Providing an alternate reaction pathway with LOWER activation energy E_a","C":"Shifting equilibrium constant K","D":"Raising ΔG"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 1, 'Catalyst lowers E_a equally for forward and reverse steps without altering ΔG or K.', NULL, NULL, 'Catalyst lowers E_a equally for forward and reverse steps without altering ΔG or K.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001e', 'Assertion (A): Molecularity of a reaction can never be zero, fractional, or negative, whereas Order of a reaction can be zero or fractional.
Reason (R): Molecularity counts the integer number of colliding molecules in an elementary step, while Order is an experimentally determined sum of concentration exponents.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 1, 'Distinguishes theoretical elementary step count from empirical rate law.', NULL, NULL, 'Distinguishes theoretical elementary step count from empirical rate law.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f', 'Distinguish between Order of a Reaction and Molecularity (two key points).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 2, 'Order: experimental quantity, can be zero/fractional, applies to both elementary & complex reactions. Molecularity: theoretical integer ≥1, applies strictly to elementary steps.', '1M × 2', NULL, 'Order: experimental quantity, can be zero/fractional, applies to both elementary & complex reactions. Molecularity: theoretical integer ≥1, applies strictly to elementary steps.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000020', 'For A + 2B -> C, rate = k[A][B]². How does initial rate change if [A] is halved and [B] is doubled?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 2, 'r'' = k(½[A])(2[B])² = (1/2)×4 r = 2r (rate doubles).', '2M', NULL, 'r'' = k(½[A])(2[B])² = (1/2)×4 r = 2r (rate doubles).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000021', 'Show that for a First-order reaction, the time required for 99.9% completion is approximately 10 times the half-life (t₁/₂) of the reaction.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 3, 't_99.9% = (2.303/k) log(100 / 0.1) = (2.303/k)(3) = 6.909/k. t₁/₂ = 0.693/k -> ratio = 6.909/0.693 ≈ 10.', 'Formula setup (1.5M) + Ratio ~10 (1.5M)', NULL, 't_99.9% = (2.303/k) log(100 / 0.1) = (2.303/k)(3) = 6.909/k. t₁/₂ = 0.693/k -> ratio = 6.909/0.693 ≈ 10.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000022', 'Rate of a reaction quadruples when temperature changes from 293 K to 313 K. Calculate Activation Energy E_a (R = 8.314 J/K mol, log 4 = 0.6021).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 3, '0.6021 = [E_a / (2.303×8.314)] × [(313-293)/(293×313)] -> E_a = (0.6021×19.147×91709)/20 = 52.86 kJ/mol.', 'Arrhenius equation (1.5M) + 52.86 kJ/mol (1.5M)', NULL, '0.6021 = [E_a / (2.303×8.314)] × [(313-293)/(293×313)] -> E_a = (0.6021×19.147×91709)/20 = 52.86 kJ/mol.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000023', 'Derive the Integrated Rate Equation for (a) a Zero-order reaction (k = ([R]₀ - [R])/t) and (b) a First-order reaction (k = (2.303/t) log([R]₀/[R])) with neat linear plots of [R] vs t and log([R]₀/[R]) vs t. Also calculate time needed for 75% decay of a first-order reaction whose t₁/₂ is 20 minutes.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 5, '(a) Zero-order integration & linear plot (1.5M); (b) First-order integration & plot (2M); (c) 75% decay = 2 half-lives = 40 minutes (1.5M).', 'Zero & 1st order (3.5M) + 40 min (1.5M)', NULL, '(a) Zero-order integration & linear plot (1.5M); (b) First-order integration & plot (2M); (c) 75% decay = 2 half-lives = 40 minutes (1.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000024', '(i) Define Effective Collisions and Collision Frequency Z in Collision Theory (k = P Z_AB e^(-E_a/RT)). (ii) What does steric factor P represent? (iii) Order of thermal decomposition of NH₃ on hot Pt surface at high pressure. (iv) A first-order reaction takes 40 min for 30% decomposition; find k.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 4, '(i) Collisions between properly oriented molecules with kinetic energy ≥ Threshold Energy leading to product formation; Z = collisions/s/unit volume. (ii) Orientation probability factor. (iii) Zero-order (surface saturated). (iv) k = (2.303/40) log(100/70) = 0.05757 × 0.1549 = 8.92 × 10⁻³ min⁻¹.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Pharmaceutical shelf-life studies model drug degradation as first-order kinetics across elevated temperatures using Arrhenius plots to certify expiry dates.', '(i) Collisions between properly oriented molecules with kinetic energy ≥ Threshold Energy leading to product formation; Z = collisions/s/unit volume. (ii) Orientation probability factor. (iii) Zero-order (surface saturated). (iv) k = (2.303/40) log(100/70) = 0.05757 × 0.1549 = 8.92 × 10⁻³ min⁻¹.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000025', 'Which 3d-series element has the lowest melting point and is NOT considered a true transition metal (alongside Cd, Hg)?', '{"A":"Scandium","B":"Zinc (completely filled 3d¹⁰ in ground and +2 oxidation states)","C":"Chromium","D":"Manganese"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 1, 'Zn, Cd, Hg lack incompletely filled d-orbitals in ground/common oxidation states.', NULL, NULL, 'Zn, Cd, Hg lack incompletely filled d-orbitals in ground/common oxidation states.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000026', 'Highest common oxidation state (+7) in the 3d transition series is exhibited by:', '{"A":"Chromium","B":"Manganese (e.g., MnO₄⁻)","C":"Iron","D":"Vanadium"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 1, 'Mn (3d⁵ 4s²) uses all 7 valence electrons.', NULL, NULL, 'Mn (3d⁵ 4s²) uses all 7 valence electrons.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000027', 'Zr (Z=40) and Hf (Z=72) possess nearly identical atomic radii (~160 pm vs 159 pm) primarily due to:', '{"A":"Inert pair effect","B":"Lanthanoid Contraction (poor shielding by 4f electrons)","C":"Shielding by d-electrons","D":"Hydrogen bonding"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 1, 'Poor shielding of 4f orbital pulls outer shells tightly.', NULL, NULL, 'Poor shielding of 4f orbital pulls outer shells tightly.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000028', 'Spin-only magnetic moment of Fe²⁺ (Z=26, 4 unpaired electrons in 3d⁶) in Bohr Magnetons is:', '{"A":"3.87 BM","B":"4.90 BM","C":"5.92 BM","D":"2.84 BM"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 1, 'μ = √(n(n+2)) = √(4×6) = √24 ≈ 4.90 BM.', NULL, NULL, 'μ = √(n(n+2)) = √(4×6) = √24 ≈ 4.90 BM.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000029', 'Most common and stable oxidation state shown by all Lanthanoids is:', '{"A":"+2","B":"+3 (e.g., Ln³⁺)","C":"+4","D":"+5"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 1, '+3 predominates; Ce⁴⁺ is good oxidising agent, Eu²⁺ good reducing agent.', NULL, NULL, '+3 predominates; Ce⁴⁺ is good oxidising agent, Eu²⁺ good reducing agent.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002a', 'Assertion (A): Copper (Cu) shows a positive standard electrode potential E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V and does NOT liberate H₂ gas from dilute acids.
Reason (R): High enthalpy of atomisation and ionisation enthalpy of Cu are not compensated by its hydration enthalpy.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 1, 'Explains Cu''s positive E° value.', NULL, NULL, 'Explains Cu''s positive E° value.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002b', 'Why do transition metals act as good catalysts (give two reasons) and form coloured ions?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 2, 'Catalysts: variable oxidation states & large surface area forming intermediate complexes. Colour: d–d electronic transitions in partially filled d-orbitals absorbing visible light.', '1M + 1M', NULL, 'Catalysts: variable oxidation states & large surface area forming intermediate complexes. Colour: d–d electronic transitions in partially filled d-orbitals absorbing visible light.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002c', 'Why is Cr²⁺ strongly reducing while Mn³⁺ is strongly oxidising (even though both are d⁴ systems)?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 2, 'Cr²⁺ -> Cr³⁺ moves to stable half-filled t₂g³ configuration; Mn³⁺ -> Mn²⁺ attains ultra-stable half-filled d⁵ configuration.', '1M each', NULL, 'Cr²⁺ -> Cr³⁺ moves to stable half-filled t₂g³ configuration; Mn³⁺ -> Mn²⁺ attains ultra-stable half-filled d⁵ configuration.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002d', 'Explain: (i) Why Sc³⁺ and Ti⁴⁺ salts are colourless; (ii) Why transition metals form interstitial compounds (give 2 properties); (iii) Why E°(Mn³⁺/Mn²⁺) is much more positive than E°(Cr³⁺/Cr²⁺).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 3, '(i) d⁰ configuration leaves zero d-electrons for d-d transition. (ii) Small H/C/N atoms trapped inside crystal lattice voids -> high m.p., very hard, retain metallic conductivity. (iii) Half-filled d⁵ stability of Mn²⁺ vs t₂g³ stability of Cr³⁺.', '1M × 3', NULL, '(i) d⁰ configuration leaves zero d-electrons for d-d transition. (ii) Small H/C/N atoms trapped inside crystal lattice voids -> high m.p., very hard, retain metallic conductivity. (iii) Half-filled d⁵ stability of Mn²⁺ vs t₂g³ stability of Cr³⁺.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002e', 'Write balanced ionic equations for oxidising action of Potassium Permanganate (KMnO₄) in acidic medium with: (i) Fe²⁺ ions, (ii) Oxalate (C₂O₄²⁻) ions, and (iii) I⁻ ions.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 3, '(i) MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ + 8H⁺ -> Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O. (ii) 2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ -> 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O. (iii) 2MnO₄⁻ + 10I⁻ + 16H⁺ -> 2Mn²⁺ + 5I₂ + 8H₂O.', '1M × 3', NULL, '(i) MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ + 8H⁺ -> Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O. (ii) 2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ -> 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O. (iii) 2MnO₄⁻ + 10I⁻ + 16H⁺ -> 2Mn²⁺ + 5I₂ + 8H₂O.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002f', 'Describe the preparation of Potassium Dichromate (K₂Cr₂O₇) from Chromite ore (FeCr₂O₄) with all 3 balanced steps, explain pH-dependent interconversion between yellow Chromate (CrO₄²⁻) and orange Dichromate (Cr₂O₇²⁻), and write equations for its oxidising reaction with H₂S and Sn²⁺. Also contrast Lanthanoid Contraction vs Actinoid Contraction.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 5, '3 prep steps (2M) + Chromate/Dichromate pH equilibrium + 2 redox reactions (2M) + Actinoid contraction greater due to even poorer 5f shielding (1M).', 'Preparation (2M) + pH & Redox (2M) + Actinoid contrast (1M)', NULL, '3 prep steps (2M) + Chromate/Dichromate pH equilibrium + 2 redox reactions (2M) + Actinoid contraction greater due to even poorer 5f shielding (1M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', '(i) Composition and one use of Mischmetall alloy. (ii) Number of unpaired electrons & magnetic moment of Mn²⁺ (Z=25). (iii) Why do Actinoids exhibit a wider range of oxidation states than Lanthanoids? (iv) Reaction of KMnO₄ with I⁻ in neutral/faintly alkaline medium.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'The d- and f-Block Elements', 4, '(i) ~95% lanthanoid metal + ~5% iron + traces of S, C, Ca, Al; used in Mg-alloy bullets/lighter flints. (ii) d⁵ -> 5 unpaired e⁻ -> √35 = 5.92 BM. (iii) Comparable energies of 5f, 6d, and 7s subshells. (iv) 2MnO₄⁻ + I⁻ + H₂O -> 2MnO₂ + IO₃⁻ + 2OH⁻.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Metallurgical steel plants rely on ferro-vanadium, chromite-derived dichromates, and Mischmetall lanthanoid alloys to tailor industrial hardness and oxidation resistance.', '(i) ~95% lanthanoid metal + ~5% iron + traces of S, C, Ca, Al; used in Mg-alloy bullets/lighter flints. (ii) d⁵ -> 5 unpaired e⁻ -> √35 = 5.92 BM. (iii) Comparable energies of 5f, 6d, and 7s subshells. (iv) 2MnO₄⁻ + I⁻ + H₂O -> 2MnO₂ + IO₃⁻ + 2OH⁻.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000031', 'IUPAC name of [Co(NH₃)₅(CO₃)]Cl is:', '{"A":"Pentaamminecarbonatocobalt(II) chloride","B":"Pentaamminecarbonatocobalt(III) chloride","C":"Carbonatopentaamminecobalt(III) chloride","D":"Pentaamminecobalt(III) carbonate chloride"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 1, 'Ligands alphabetical: ammine before carbonato; Co = +3.', NULL, NULL, 'Ligands alphabetical: ammine before carbonato; Co = +3.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000032', 'Which complex shows Geometrical (cis-trans) isomerism as well as Optical isomerism in its cis-form?', '{"A":"[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]","B":"[Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺","C":"[Ni(CO)₄]","D":"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 1, 'cis-[Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺ lacks a plane of symmetry (optically active d & l enantiomers).', NULL, NULL, 'cis-[Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺ lacks a plane of symmetry (optically active d & l enantiomers).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000033', 'Using Crystal Field Theory, [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ is diamagnetic because CN⁻ is a strong-field ligand giving configuration:', '{"A":"t₂g⁴ e_g²","B":"t₂g⁶ e_g⁰ (low-spin inner orbital d²sp³)","C":"t₂g³ e_g³","D":"e² t₂⁴"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 1, 'Δ₀ > P pairs all six d-electrons in t₂g⁶.', NULL, NULL, 'Δ₀ > P pairs all six d-electrons in t₂g⁶.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000034', 'Number of chloride ions precipitated immediately by excess AgNO₃ from 1 mole of [Cr(H₂O)₅Cl]Cl₂ is:', '{"A":"3 moles","B":"2 moles (only outer counter-ion Cl⁻)","C":"1 mole","D":"Zero"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 1, '2 ionisable Cl⁻ outside coordination sphere.', NULL, NULL, '2 ionisable Cl⁻ outside coordination sphere.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000035', 'Hybridisation and shape of [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ vs [NiCl₄]²⁻ respectively are:', '{"A":"sp³ tetrahedral and dsp² square planar","B":"dsp² square planar (diamagnetic) and sp³ tetrahedral (paramagnetic)","C":"Both sp³","D":"Both dsp²"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 1, 'Strong CN⁻ pairs 3d⁸ giving dsp²; weak Cl⁻ leaves 2 unpaired e⁻ giving sp³.', NULL, NULL, 'Strong CN⁻ pairs 3d⁸ giving dsp²; weak Cl⁻ leaves 2 unpaired e⁻ giving sp³.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000036', 'Assertion (A): Chelate complexes (containing polydentate ligands like ethylenediamine or EDTA) are considerably more stable than complexes with monodentate ligands.
Reason (R): Ring formation increases entropy favourably upon replacing multiple monodentate solvent molecules (Chelate effect).
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 1, 'Positive ΔS drives stability of chelates.', NULL, NULL, 'Positive ΔS drives stability of chelates.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000037', 'Distinguish between a Double Salt (Mohr''s salt / Carnallite) and a Coordination Complex (K₄[Fe(CN)₆]).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 2, 'Double salt dissociates completely into simple ions in water; Complex retains its discrete complex ion [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ intact without giving Fe²⁺ or CN⁻ tests.', '1M + 1M', NULL, 'Double salt dissociates completely into simple ions in water; Complex retains its discrete complex ion [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ intact without giving Fe²⁺ or CN⁻ tests.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000038', 'Write formulas and examples of Ionisation Isomerism and Linkage Isomerism.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 2, 'Ionisation: [Co(NH₃)₅(SO₄)]Br and [Co(NH₃)₅Br]SO₄. Linkage (ambidentate NO₂⁻/SCN⁻): [Co(NH₃)₅(NO₂)]Cl₂ and [Co(NH₃)₅(ONO)]Cl₂.', '1M each', NULL, 'Ionisation: [Co(NH₃)₅(SO₄)]Br and [Co(NH₃)₅Br]SO₄. Linkage (ambidentate NO₂⁻/SCN⁻): [Co(NH₃)₅(NO₂)]Cl₂ and [Co(NH₃)₅(ONO)]Cl₂.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000039', 'Using Valence Bond Theory (VBT), predict hybridisation, geometry, and magnetic behaviour of (i) [CoF₆]³⁻ and (ii) [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 3, '(i) Co³⁺ (3d⁶), weak F⁻ doesn''t pair -> sp³d² outer-orbital octahedral, 4 unpaired e⁻ (paramagnetic). (ii) Strong NH₃ pairs -> d²sp³ inner-orbital octahedral, 0 unpaired e⁻ (diamagnetic).', '1.5M each', NULL, '(i) Co³⁺ (3d⁶), weak F⁻ doesn''t pair -> sp³d² outer-orbital octahedral, 4 unpaired e⁻ (paramagnetic). (ii) Strong NH₃ pairs -> d²sp³ inner-orbital octahedral, 0 unpaired e⁻ (diamagnetic).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003a', 'Draw the Crystal Field Splitting diagrams of d-orbitals in an Octahedral field vs a Tetrahedral field (showing Δ_t = 4/9 Δ₀).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 3, 'Octahedral: lower t₂g (-0.4 Δ₀) and upper e_g (+0.6 Δ₀). Tetrahedral inverted: lower e and upper t₂ with smaller splitting Δ_t = (4/9) Δ₀ (no low-spin tetrahedral complexes).', '1.5M each', NULL, 'Octahedral: lower t₂g (-0.4 Δ₀) and upper e_g (+0.6 Δ₀). Tetrahedral inverted: lower e and upper t₂ with smaller splitting Δ_t = (4/9) Δ₀ (no low-spin tetrahedral complexes).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003b', 'Explain Werner''s Theory of Coordination Compounds (primary vs secondary valencies). Explain Synergic Bonding in Metal Carbonyls like [Ni(CO)₄] and [Fe(CO)₅] (M<-CO σ bond + M->CO π back-bonding), and mention three applications of coordination compounds in medicine (Cisplatin, EDTA), water hardness estimation, and metallurgy.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 5, 'Werner postulates (2M) + Synergic σ-π bonding in metal carbonyls (1.5M) + 3 applications (Cisplatin cancer therapy, EDTA lead poisoning/Ca²⁺-Mg²⁺ titration, Cyanide Au/Ag extraction) (1.5M).', 'Werner (2M) + Carbonyl bonding (1.5M) + 3 applications (1.5M)', NULL, 'Werner postulates (2M) + Synergic σ-π bonding in metal carbonyls (1.5M) + 3 applications (Cisplatin cancer therapy, EDTA lead poisoning/Ca²⁺-Mg²⁺ titration, Cyanide Au/Ag extraction) (1.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003c', '(i) Spectrochemical series: strong vs weak ligand impact on colour of [Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺ upon heating (water loss). (ii) Calculate CFSE for d⁵ high-spin vs d⁶ low-spin octahedral complex in Δ₀ units. (iii) Coordination number of Fe in K₃[Fe(C₂O₄)₃]. (iv) Why is [Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺ violet whereas [Sc(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is colourless?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 4, '(i) Removing H₂O ligands eliminates d-orbital crystal field splitting -> crystal turns colourless. (ii) d⁵ high spin: 3(-0.4)+2(+0.6) = 0; d⁶ low spin: 6(-0.4)+3P = -2.4 Δ₀ + 3P (or -2.4 Δ₀ + 2P pairing extra). (iii) 3 × 2 = 6 (oxalate is bidentate). (iv) Ti³⁺ has 3d¹ (d-d transition possible); Sc³⁺ has 3d⁰.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Clinical labs measure serum calcium with EDTA chelators while catalytic synthesis exploits transition-metal carbonyls and stereospecific platinum complexes.', '(i) Removing H₂O ligands eliminates d-orbital crystal field splitting -> crystal turns colourless. (ii) d⁵ high spin: 3(-0.4)+2(+0.6) = 0; d⁶ low spin: 6(-0.4)+3P = -2.4 Δ₀ + 3P (or -2.4 Δ₀ + 2P pairing extra). (iii) 3 × 2 = 6 (oxalate is bidentate). (iv) Ti³⁺ has 3d¹ (d-d transition possible); Sc³⁺ has 3d⁰.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003d', 'Which mechanism proceeds with 100% Walden Inversion of stereochemical configuration?', '{"A":"S_N1","B":"S_N2 (backside nucleophilic attack in one concerted step)","C":"E1","D":"Free radical"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 1, 'Bimolecular S_N2 attacks 180° opposite the leaving group.', NULL, NULL, 'Bimolecular S_N2 attacks 180° opposite the leaving group.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003e', 'Correct order of S_N1 reactivity among alkyl halides is:', '{"A":"Methyl > 1° > 2° > 3°","B":"3° > 2° > 1° > Methyl","C":"1° > 2° > 3°","D":"Aryl > Vinyl > 3°"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 1, 'Carbocation stability 3° > 2° > 1° governs S_N1 rate.', NULL, NULL, 'Carbocation stability 3° > 2° > 1° governs S_N1 rate.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003f', 'Chlorobenzene is far less reactive than chloroethane toward nucleophilic substitution because of:', '{"A":"Higher molecular mass","B":"Resonance giving partial double-bond C–Cl character, sp² C–Cl strength, and phenyl cation instability","C":"Inductive effect only","D":"Free radicals"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 1, 'Four key NCERT reasons make aryl halides unreactive under mild conditions.', NULL, NULL, 'Four key NCERT reasons make aryl halides unreactive under mild conditions.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000040', 'Heating alkyl chloride with AgCN produces predominantly:', '{"A":"Alkyl cyanide (R-CN)","B":"Alkyl isocyanide (R-NC) due to covalent Ag-CN bonding","C":"Alcohol","D":"Alkane"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 1, 'Ionic KCN gives R-CN; covalent AgCN leaves N lone pair free -> R-NC.', NULL, NULL, 'Ionic KCN gives R-CN; covalent AgCN leaves N lone pair free -> R-NC.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000041', 'Mixture of Chloroform and Acetone? Better: Phosgene (poisonous COCl₂) forms when chloroform is exposed to air & sunlight; hence CHCl₃ is stored in:', '{"A":"Clear glass bottles open to air","B":"Dark coloured bottles filled to the brim (often with ~1% ethanol)","C":"Plastic jugs","D":"Aluminium cans"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 1, 'Dark bottles exclude light; brim-filling excludes O₂; ethanol converts trace COCl₂ to harmless diethyl carbonate.', NULL, NULL, 'Dark bottles exclude light; brim-filling excludes O₂; ethanol converts trace COCl₂ to harmless diethyl carbonate.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000042', 'Assertion (A): Optically active 2-bromobutane undergoes racemisation when hydrolysed via S_N1 mechanism.
Reason (R): Planar sp² carbocation intermediate allows equal nucleophile attack from both front and back faces, forming a 50:50 d/l racemic mixture.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 1, 'Planar carbocation causes racemisation.', NULL, NULL, 'Planar carbocation causes racemisation.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000043', 'Arrange in increasing order of boiling points: Bromoform, Chloromethane, Dibromomethane, Bromomethane.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 2, 'CH₃Cl < CH₃Br < CH₂Br₂ < CHBr₃ (boiling point rises with atomic mass and size of halogen).', 'Order (1M) + van der Waals reason (1M)', NULL, 'CH₃Cl < CH₃Br < CH₂Br₂ < CHBr₃ (boiling point rises with atomic mass and size of halogen).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000044', 'Write balanced equations for (i) Finkelstein reaction and (ii) Swarts reaction.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 2, '(i) R-X + NaI --Dry Acetone--> R-I + NaX. (ii) CH₃-Br + AgF -> CH₃-F + AgBr.', '1M each', NULL, '(i) R-X + NaI --Dry Acetone--> R-I + NaX. (ii) CH₃-Br + AgF -> CH₃-F + AgBr.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000045', 'Compare S_N1 and S_N2 mechanisms across Kinetics (order), Stereochemistry, Intermediate, and Preferred alkyl halide.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 3, 'S_N1: 1st order rate=k[RX], racemisation, carbocation intermediate, 3°>2°>1°. S_N2: 2nd order rate=k[RX][Nu], inversion of configuration, pentacoordinate TS (no intermediate), Methyl>1°>2°>3°.', '3M', NULL, 'S_N1: 1st order rate=k[RX], racemisation, carbocation intermediate, 3°>2°>1°. S_N2: 2nd order rate=k[RX][Nu], inversion of configuration, pentacoordinate TS (no intermediate), Methyl>1°>2°>3°.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000046', 'Give reasons: (i) p-Dichlorobenzene has higher melting point than o- and m-isomers; (ii) Alkyl halides, though polar, are immiscible in water; (iii) Grignard reagents must be prepared under strictly anhydrous conditions.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 3, '(i) Symmetrical para structure packs tightly in crystal lattice. (ii) Cannot break strong H₂O-H₂O hydrogen bonds effectively. (iii) React violently with trace water/proton donors forming alkanes (R-MgX + H₂O -> R-H + Mg(OH)X).', '1M × 3', NULL, '(i) Symmetrical para structure packs tightly in crystal lattice. (ii) Cannot break strong H₂O-H₂O hydrogen bonds effectively. (iii) React violently with trace water/proton donors forming alkanes (R-MgX + H₂O -> R-H + Mg(OH)X).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000047', 'State Zaitsev (Saytzeff) rule with 2-bromopentane dehydrohalogenation using alcoholic KOH. Write equations for: (a) Sandmeyer reaction from benzene diazonium chloride to chlorobenzene, (b) Wurtz-Fittig reaction, (c) Friedel-Crafts acylation of chlorobenzene, and (d) reaction of chlorobenzene with NaOH at 623 K, 300 atm (Dow''s process).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 5, 'Zaitsev rule & 2-pentene major product (1.5M) + 4 named reactions cleanly balanced (3.5M).', 'Zaitsev (1.5M) + 4 named reactions (3.5M)', NULL, 'Zaitsev rule & 2-pentene major product (1.5M) + 4 named reactions cleanly balanced (3.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000048', '(i) Define Enantiomers and Racemic mixture. (ii) Out of C₆H₅CH₂Cl and C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Cl, which hydrolyses faster by S_N1 and why? (iii) Identify chiral carbon in Butan-2-ol. (iv) Major product when 1-bromopropane reacts with (a) aq KOH vs (b) alc KOH.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes', 4, '(i) Non-superimposable mirror-image stereoisomers; equimolar d+l optically inactive mixture. (ii) C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Cl (resonance-stabilised diphenylmethyl carbocation vs benzyl). (iii) C-2 attached to -H, -OH, -CH₃, -C₂H₅. (iv) (a) Propan-1-ol (substitution); (b) Propene (elimination).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Pharmaceutical synthesis separates single-enantiomer chiral intermediates using polarimeter verification and stereoselective S_N2 reactions.', '(i) Non-superimposable mirror-image stereoisomers; equimolar d+l optically inactive mixture. (ii) C₆H₅CH(C₆H₅)Cl (resonance-stabilised diphenylmethyl carbocation vs benzyl). (iii) C-2 attached to -H, -OH, -CH₃, -C₂H₅. (iv) (a) Propan-1-ol (substitution); (b) Propene (elimination).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000049', 'Acidic strength order among Ethanol, Water, and Phenol is:', '{"A":"Ethanol > Water > Phenol","B":"Phenol > Water > Ethanol","C":"Water > Ethanol > Phenol","D":"Phenol > Ethanol > Water"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 1, 'Resonance-stabilised phenoxide ion makes Phenol far stronger acid (pK_a ~10) than Water/Ethanol (~16).', NULL, NULL, 'Resonance-stabilised phenoxide ion makes Phenol far stronger acid (pK_a ~10) than Water/Ethanol (~16).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004a', 'Lucas reagent (conc. HCl + anhydrous ZnCl₂) gives immediate turbidity at room temperature with:', '{"A":"Primary (1°) alcohol","B":"Secondary (2°) alcohol","C":"Tertiary (3°) alcohol","D":"Phenol"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 1, 'Stable 3° carbocation precipitates insoluble R-Cl instantly (2° in ~5 mins; 1° none at RT).', NULL, NULL, 'Stable 3° carbocation precipitates insoluble R-Cl instantly (2° in ~5 mins; 1° none at RT).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004b', 'Williamson synthesis of tert-butyl methyl ether ((CH₃)₃C-O-CH₃) MUST use:', '{"A":"Sodium methoxide + tert-butyl bromide","B":"Sodium tert-butoxide + methyl bromide (bromomethane)","C":"Methanol + H₂SO₄","D":"tert-butanol + alc KOH"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 1, 'Using 3° halide causes E2 elimination forming 2-methylpropene; always take 1° halide + 3° alkoxide.', NULL, NULL, 'Using 3° halide causes E2 elimination forming 2-methylpropene; always take 1° halide + 3° alkoxide.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004c', 'Reaction of Phenol with Bromine water (Br₂/H₂O) gives white precipitate of:', '{"A":"o-Bromophenol","B":"2,4,6-Tribromophenol","C":"m-Bromophenol","D":"Bromobenzene"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 1, 'Polar water ionises phenol to high-activating phenoxide giving trisubstitution.', NULL, NULL, 'Polar water ionises phenol to high-activating phenoxide giving trisubstitution.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004d', 'Cumene (isopropylbenzene) on aerial oxidation followed by acidic hydrolysis industrially manufactures:', '{"A":"Methanol and Acetic acid","B":"Phenol and Acetone (propanone)","C":"Aniline","D":"Benzaldehyde"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 1, 'Most economical global industrial route for phenol + acetone.', NULL, NULL, 'Most economical global industrial route for phenol + acetone.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004e', 'Assertion (A): o-Nitrophenol is steam volatile whereas p-nitrophenol has a much higher boiling point and is not steam volatile.
Reason (R): o-Nitrophenol exhibits intramolecular hydrogen bonding within a single molecule, whereas p-nitrophenol forms extensive intermolecular H-bonding associating molecules.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 1, 'Intramolecular vs intermolecular H-bonding explains separation by steam distillation.', NULL, NULL, 'Intramolecular vs intermolecular H-bonding explains separation by steam distillation.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004f', 'Convert: (i) Propene -> Propan-2-ol and (ii) Phenol -> Picric acid (2,4,6-trinitrophenol).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 2, '(i) Hydration H⁺/H₂O (Markovnikov) -> Propan-2-ol. (ii) Conc. HNO₃ (+ conc. H₂SO₄) -> 2,4,6-trinitrophenol.', '1M each', NULL, '(i) Hydration H⁺/H₂O (Markovnikov) -> Propan-2-ol. (ii) Conc. HNO₃ (+ conc. H₂SO₄) -> 2,4,6-trinitrophenol.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000050', 'Explain why alcohols have higher boiling points than hydrocarbons and ethers of comparable molecular mass.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 2, 'Alcohols form strong intermolecular O-H···O hydrogen bonds requiring extra thermal energy to vaporise.', 'Intermolecular H-bonding (2M)', NULL, 'Alcohols form strong intermolecular O-H···O hydrogen bonds requiring extra thermal energy to vaporise.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000051', 'Write the step-by-step Mechanism of Acid-Catalysed Dehydration of Ethanol to Ethene at 443 K (3 steps: protonation, carbocation formation, deprotonation).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 3, 'CH₃CH₂OH + H⁺ -> CH₃CH₂OH₂⁺ -> CH₃CH₂⁺ + H₂O -> CH₂=CH₂ + H⁺.', '3 clear steps × 1M', NULL, 'CH₃CH₂OH + H⁺ -> CH₃CH₂OH₂⁺ -> CH₃CH₂⁺ + H₂O -> CH₂=CH₂ + H⁺.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000052', 'Write equations for: (i) Kolbe''s Reaction, (ii) Reimer-Tiemann Reaction, and (iii) Phenol heated with Zinc dust.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 3, '(i) Phenoxide + CO₂ (400 K, 4-7 atm) then H⁺ -> Salicylic acid. (ii) Phenol + CHCl₃ + aq NaOH then H⁺ -> Salicylaldehyde. (iii) Phenol + Zn -> Benzene + ZnO.', '1M × 3', NULL, '(i) Phenoxide + CO₂ (400 K, 4-7 atm) then H⁺ -> Salicylic acid. (ii) Phenol + CHCl₃ + aq NaOH then H⁺ -> Salicylaldehyde. (iii) Phenol + Zn -> Benzene + ZnO.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000053', 'Explain how 1°, 2°, and 3° alcohols are distinguished by (a) Lucas Test and (b) Heated Cu at 573 K (dehydrogenation vs dehydration). Show how anisole reacts with HI (373 K) and with Br₂ in ethanoic acid. Why does cleavage of C₆H₅-O-CH₃ by HI always yield Phenol + CH₃I (never iodobenzene + CH₃OH)?', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 5, 'Lucas + Cu 573K (1°->aldehyde, 2°->ketone, 3°->alkene!) (2.5M). Anisole reactions (1.5M) + Partial double bond C(sp²)-O resistance prevents S_N2 cleavage at aryl ring (1M).', 'Distinction tests (2.5M) + Anisole & reason (2.5M)', NULL, 'Lucas + Cu 573K (1°->aldehyde, 2°->ketone, 3°->alkene!) (2.5M). Anisole reactions (1.5M) + Partial double bond C(sp²)-O resistance prevents S_N2 cleavage at aryl ring (1M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000054', '(i) Convert Salicylic acid to Aspirin (2-acetoxybenzoic acid) with equation. (ii) Industrial preparation of Ethanol from molasses fermentation enzymes (Invertase & Zymase). (iii) Arrange p-nitrophenol, phenol, and p-cresol in increasing order of acidity. (iv) Product of Ethanol with Cu at 573 K vs 413 K with conc. H₂SO₄.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers', 4, '(i) Salicylic acid + (CH₃CO)₂O / H⁺ -> Aspirin + CH₃COOH. (ii) Sucrose --Invertase--> Glucose+Fructose --Zymase--> Ethanol + CO₂. (iii) p-Cresol < Phenol < p-Nitrophenol. (iv) Ethanal (573K Cu); Ethoxyethane (413K H₂SO₄).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A fine-chemicals plant produces commercial phenol via cumene oxidation, upgrades salicylic acid into aspirin, and purifies ortho/para nitrophenols by steam distillation.', '(i) Salicylic acid + (CH₃CO)₂O / H⁺ -> Aspirin + CH₃COOH. (ii) Sucrose --Invertase--> Glucose+Fructose --Zymase--> Ethanol + CO₂. (iii) p-Cresol < Phenol < p-Nitrophenol. (iv) Ethanal (573K Cu); Ethoxyethane (413K H₂SO₄).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000055', 'Which compound undergoes Cannizzaro reaction (disproportionation with 50% NaOH) because it lacks an α-hydrogen?', '{"A":"Ethanal","B":"Methanal (HCHO) and Benzaldehyde (C₆H₅CHO)","C":"Propanone","D":"Propanal"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 1, 'Aldehydes without α-H undergo self-oxidation/reduction in conc. alkali.', NULL, NULL, 'Aldehydes without α-H undergo self-oxidation/reduction in conc. alkali.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000056', 'Fehling''s solution gives a reddish-brown precipitate of Cu₂O with:', '{"A":"Benzaldehyde","B":"Aliphatic aldehydes like Ethanal (CH₃CHO)","C":"Acetone","D":"Acetic acid"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 1, 'Aromatic aldehydes (benzaldehyde) and ketones do NOT reduce Fehling''s solution (Tollens reacts with both aliphatic & aromatic aldehydes).', NULL, NULL, 'Aromatic aldehydes (benzaldehyde) and ketones do NOT reduce Fehling''s solution (Tollens reacts with both aliphatic & aromatic aldehydes).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000057', 'Iodoform test (yellow precipitate CHI₃ with I₂ + NaOH) is positive for compounds containing:', '{"A":"CH₃-CO- or CH₃-CH(OH)- group (e.g., Propanone, Ethanal, Ethanol)","B":"Benzaldehyde","C":"Methanol","D":"Methanoic acid"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 1, 'Methyl ketones & ethanol give positive yellow CHI₃ test.', NULL, NULL, 'Methyl ketones & ethanol give positive yellow CHI₃ test.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000058', 'Clemmensen reduction and Wolff-Kishner reduction both convert >C=O cleanly to:', '{"A":">CH-OH","B":"-CH₂- (alkane)","C":"-COOH","D":"Ester"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 1, 'Zn-Hg/conc. HCl (acidic Clemmensen); NH₂NH₂/KOH-ethylene glycol (basic Wolff-Kishner).', NULL, NULL, 'Zn-Hg/conc. HCl (acidic Clemmensen); NH₂NH₂/KOH-ethylene glycol (basic Wolff-Kishner).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000059', 'Correct increasing order of acid strength is:', '{"A":"FCH₂COOH < ClCH₂COOH < CH₃COOH","B":"CH₃COOH < HCOOH < ClCH₂COOH < FCH₂COOH","C":"ClCH₂COOH < FCH₂COOH < HCOOH","D":"HCOOH < CH₃COOH"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 1, '-I electron-withdrawing substituents stabilise carboxylate anion lowering pK_a.', NULL, NULL, '-I electron-withdrawing substituents stabilise carboxylate anion lowering pK_a.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005a', 'Assertion (A): Aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones toward nucleophilic addition reactions.
Reason (R): In ketones, two alkyl groups exert stronger steric hindrance and +I electron donation reducing electrophilicity of carbonyl carbon compared to one alkyl group in aldehydes.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 1, 'Both steric and electronic factors favour aldehydes.', NULL, NULL, 'Both steric and electronic factors favour aldehydes.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005b', 'How do you chemically distinguish between (i) Propanal and Propanone, and (ii) Benzoic acid and Ethyl benzoate?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 2, '(i) Tollens'' reagent gives silver mirror only with Propanal (or Iodoform gives yellow ppt only with Propanone). (ii) NaHCO₃ gives brisk CO₂ effervescence only with Benzoic acid.', '1M each', NULL, '(i) Tollens'' reagent gives silver mirror only with Propanal (or Iodoform gives yellow ppt only with Propanone). (ii) NaHCO₃ gives brisk CO₂ effervescence only with Benzoic acid.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005c', 'Why does Carboxylic acid NOT give characteristic nucleophilic addition reactions of carbonyl (>C=O) group like aldehydes/ketones?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 2, 'Lone pair of -OH oxygen resonates into C=O reducing partial positive electrophilicity of carbonyl carbon.', 'Resonance delocalisation reason (2M)', NULL, 'Lone pair of -OH oxygen resonates into C=O reducing partial positive electrophilicity of carbonyl carbon.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005d', 'Write balanced equations for: (i) Rosenmund Reduction, (ii) Etard Reaction, and (iii) Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky (HVZ) Reaction.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 3, '(i) R-COCl + H₂ --Pd/BaSO₄--> R-CHO + HCl. (ii) Toluene + CrO₂Cl₂/CS₂ then H₃O⁺ -> Benzaldehyde. (iii) R-CH₂-COOH + X₂ / Red P then H₂O -> α-halocarboxylic acid.', '1M × 3', NULL, '(i) R-COCl + H₂ --Pd/BaSO₄--> R-CHO + HCl. (ii) Toluene + CrO₂Cl₂/CS₂ then H₃O⁺ -> Benzaldehyde. (iii) R-CH₂-COOH + X₂ / Red P then H₂O -> α-halocarboxylic acid.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005e', 'Explain Aldol Condensation with Ethanal giving structure of 3-hydroxybutanal (aldol) and But-2-enal.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 3, '2 CH₃CHO --dil NaOH--> CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CHO --Heat--> CH₃CH=CHCHO + H₂O.', 'Aldol step (1.5M) + Dehydration product (1.5M)', NULL, '2 CH₃CHO --dil NaOH--> CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CHO --Heat--> CH₃CH=CHCHO + H₂O.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005f', 'Account for: (a) Boiling points of aldehydes/ketones > hydrocarbons but < alcohols of similar mass; (b) Aromatic carboxylic acids (benzoic acid) do NOT undergo Friedel-Crafts alkylation; (c) Methanoic acid reduces Tollens'' reagent unlike other carboxylic acids. Give conversions: (i) Ethanal -> But-2-enoic acid, (ii) Benzene -> m-Nitroacetophenone.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 5, '(a) Dipole-dipole vs no H-bond; (b) Deactivating -COOH + Lewis acid AlCl₃ binds -COOH oxygen; (c) HCOOH contains an aldehydic -CHO moiety (3M). (i) Aldol -> Crotonaldehyde -> Tollens oxidation; (ii) Friedel-Crafts acetylation -> Nitration HNO₃/H₂SO₄ (2M).', '3 reasons (3M) + 2 multi-step conversions (2M)', NULL, '(a) Dipole-dipole vs no H-bond; (b) Deactivating -COOH + Lewis acid AlCl₃ binds -COOH oxygen; (c) HCOOH contains an aldehydic -CHO moiety (3M). (i) Aldol -> Crotonaldehyde -> Tollens oxidation; (ii) Friedel-Crafts acetylation -> Nitration HNO₃/H₂SO₄ (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000060', '(i) Reagent used in Stephen reaction converting nitriles R-CN to aldehydes. (ii) Product when Benzaldehyde + Acetophenone warm in dilute NaOH (Cross-Aldol). (iii) Arrange HCHO, CH₃CHO, CH₃COCH₃ in decreasing reactivity toward HCN addition. (iv) Carboxylic acid dimers exist in vapour/aprotic benzene due to?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids', 4, '(i) SnCl₂ + HCl followed by hydrolysis (or DIBAL-H). (ii) Benzalacetophenone (C₆H₅-CH=CH-CO-C₆H₅). (iii) HCHO > CH₃CHO > CH₃COCH₃. (iv) Strong cyclic intermolecular hydrogen bonding (apparent molar mass doubles).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A medicinal chemistry lab differentiates closely boiling isomers using Tollens'' silver mirror, NaHCO₃ effervescence, and iodoform yellow precipitates before multi-step synthesis.', '(i) SnCl₂ + HCl followed by hydrolysis (or DIBAL-H). (ii) Benzalacetophenone (C₆H₅-CH=CH-CO-C₆H₅). (iii) HCHO > CH₃CHO > CH₃COCH₃. (iv) Strong cyclic intermolecular hydrogen bonding (apparent molar mass doubles).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000061', 'Gabriel Phthalimide synthesis exclusively prepares pure:', '{"A":"Aromatic primary amines (Aniline)","B":"Aliphatic primary amines (R-NH₂) without 2°/3° contamination","C":"Secondary amines","D":"Tertiary amines"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 1, 'Aryl halides do not undergo S_N2 with potassium phthalimide.', NULL, NULL, 'Aryl halides do not undergo S_N2 with potassium phthalimide.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000062', 'In Hoffmann Bromamide Degradation reaction (R-CONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH -> R-NH₂), the amine formed contains:', '{"A":"Same carbon count","B":"One carbon atom LESS than the parent amide","C":"One carbon atom more","D":"No nitrogen"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 1, 'Steps down the homologous series by 1 carbon.', NULL, NULL, 'Steps down the homologous series by 1 carbon.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000063', 'In aqueous solution, the correct decreasing order of basic strength among ethyl-substituted amines is:', '{"A":"(C₂H₅)₃N > (C₂H₅)₂NH > C₂H₅NH₂ > NH₃","B":"(C₂H₅)₂NH > (C₂H₅)₃N > C₂H₅NH₂ > NH₃","C":"C₂H₅NH₂ > (C₂H₅)₂NH > (C₂H₅)₃N","D":"NH₃ > C₂H₅NH₂"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 1, 'Combined +I, solvation (H-bonding), and steric effects: 2° > 3° > 1° > NH₃ (for ethyl; for methyl 2° > 1° > 3° > NH₃).', NULL, NULL, 'Combined +I, solvation (H-bonding), and steric effects: 2° > 3° > 1° > NH₃ (for ethyl; for methyl 2° > 1° > 3° > NH₃).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000064', 'Carbylamine test (heating with CHCl₃ + alc KOH yielding foul-smelling isocyanide R-NC) is given ONLY by:', '{"A":"Primary (1°) aliphatic and aromatic amines","B":"Secondary (2°) amines","C":"Tertiary (3°) amines","D":"Quaternary salts"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 1, 'Distinguishes 1° amines from 2° and 3° amines.', NULL, NULL, 'Distinguishes 1° amines from 2° and 3° amines.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000065', 'Hinsberg''s reagent used to distinguish 1°, 2°, and 3° amines is:', '{"A":"Acetyl chloride","B":"Benzenesulphonyl chloride (C₆H₅SO₂Cl)","C":"Nitrous acid","D":"Bromine water"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 1, '1° forms alkali-soluble sulphonamide; 2° forms alkali-insoluble; 3° doesn''t react.', NULL, NULL, '1° forms alkali-soluble sulphonamide; 2° forms alkali-insoluble; 3° doesn''t react.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000066', 'Assertion (A): Aniline does NOT undergo Friedel-Crafts alkylation or acylation with AlCl₃.
Reason (R): Aniline is a Lewis base and reacts with acidic AlCl₃ catalyst to form a salt ([C₆H₅NH₃]⁺AlCl₄⁻), making N positively charged and strongly deactivating the ring.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 1, 'Catalyst poisoning creates strongly meta-directing salt.', NULL, NULL, 'Catalyst poisoning creates strongly meta-directing salt.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000067', 'Why is Aniline a much weaker base (higher pK_b) than Methylamine and Ammonia?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 2, 'N lone pair in aniline delocalises into the benzene ring across resonance structures, making it less available for protonation (and anilinium ion lacks resonance stabilisation).', 'Resonance delocalisation (2M)', NULL, 'N lone pair in aniline delocalises into the benzene ring across resonance structures, making it less available for protonation (and anilinium ion lacks resonance stabilisation).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000068', 'Why does direct nitration of Aniline yield 47% meta-nitroaniline alongside para (51%) and ortho (2%)?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 2, 'In strongly acidic HNO₃/H₂SO₄, aniline protonates into anilinium ion (-NH₃⁺), which is strongly meta-directing.', 'Anilinium -NH₃⁺ meta-direction (2M)', NULL, 'In strongly acidic HNO₃/H₂SO₄, aniline protonates into anilinium ion (-NH₃⁺), which is strongly meta-directing.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000069', 'How are mono-bromo (p-bromoaniline) and controlled nitration (p-nitroaniline) obtained selectively from Aniline without trisubstitution or meta interference?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 3, 'Protect -NH₂ first by acetylation with (CH₃CO)₂O/pyridine forming Acetanilide (moderates activating power & prevents protonation), perform bromination/nitration -> para major -> hydrolyse with H⁺/OH⁻ back to -NH₂.', 'Acetylation protection (1.5M) + Hydrolysis steps (1.5M)', NULL, 'Protect -NH₂ first by acetylation with (CH₃CO)₂O/pyridine forming Acetanilide (moderates activating power & prevents protonation), perform bromination/nitration -> para major -> hydrolyse with H⁺/OH⁻ back to -NH₂.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006a', 'Explain Hinsberg''s Test with reactions distinguishing Ethanamine (1°), N-Ethylethanamine (2°), and N,N-Diethylethanamine (3°).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 3, 'C₆H₅SO₂Cl: 1° gives N-ethylbenzenesulphonamide (acidic H on N dissolves in KOH); 2° gives N,N-diethylbenzenesulphonamide (no acidic H, insoluble in KOH); 3° shows no reaction.', '1M × 3', NULL, 'C₆H₅SO₂Cl: 1° gives N-ethylbenzenesulphonamide (acidic H on N dissolves in KOH); 2° gives N,N-diethylbenzenesulphonamide (no acidic H, insoluble in KOH); 3° shows no reaction.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006b', 'Show how Benzene Diazonium Chloride (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻) is prepared from Aniline at 273–278 K (Diazotisation). Write balanced equations to convert Benzene Diazonium Chloride into: (i) Phenol, (ii) Iodobenzene, (iii) Benzonitrile (C₆H₅CN), (iv) Benzene (using H₃PO₂/C₂H₅OH), and (v) p-Hydroxyazobenzene (orange dye coupling reaction).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 5, 'Diazotisation equation (1M) + 5 clean conversions (warm H₂O, KI, CuCN/KCN, H₃PO₂+H₂O, Phenol in weak OH⁻) (4M).', 'Diazotisation (1M) + 5 conversions (4M)', NULL, 'Diazotisation equation (1M) + 5 clean conversions (warm H₂O, KI, CuCN/KCN, H₃PO₂+H₂O, Phenol in weak OH⁻) (4M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006c', '(i) Convert Aniline -> 1,3,5-Tribromobenzene? Or Aniline -> Benzoic acid. (ii) Explain why aliphatic amines are soluble in water while aniline is nearly insoluble. (iii) Reacts CH₃NH₂ with HNO₂ vs C₆H₅NH₂ with HNO₂ at 273 K. (iv) Arrange CH₃NH₂, (CH₃)₂NH, (CH₃)₃N in decreasing basicity in gas phase vs aqueous phase.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 4, '(i) Diazotise -> CuCN -> C₆H₅CN -> H⁺/H₂O -> C₆H₅COOH. (ii) Small aliphatic amines H-bond strongly with H₂O; bulky hydrophobic phenyl ring lowers solubility. (iii) CH₃NH₂ gives CH₃OH + N₂↑ quantitatively; C₆H₅NH₂ gives cold stable C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻. (iv) Gas: 3° > 2° > 1°; Aqueous: 2° > 1° > 3°.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Azo dye factories prepare bright orange and yellow chromophores at ice-cold temperatures (0–5°C) by coupling stable aromatic diazonium salts with activated phenols and anilines.', '(i) Diazotise -> CuCN -> C₆H₅CN -> H⁺/H₂O -> C₆H₅COOH. (ii) Small aliphatic amines H-bond strongly with H₂O; bulky hydrophobic phenyl ring lowers solubility. (iii) CH₃NH₂ gives CH₃OH + N₂↑ quantitatively; C₆H₅NH₂ gives cold stable C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻. (iv) Gas: 3° > 2° > 1°; Aqueous: 2° > 1° > 3°.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006d', 'Which disaccharide is a NON-reducing sugar because both anomeric carbons (C1 glucose & C2 fructose) are locked in glycosidic linkage?', '{"A":"Maltose","B":"Lactose","C":"Sucrose (Cane sugar)","D":"Cellobiose"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 1, 'Sucrose lacks a free hemiacetal aldehyde/ketone group (negative Fehling/Tollens).', NULL, NULL, 'Sucrose lacks a free hemiacetal aldehyde/ketone group (negative Fehling/Tollens).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006e', 'α-D-(+)-Glucose and β-D-(+)-Glucose differ ONLY in spatial configuration at C-1 and are called:', '{"A":"Epimers only","B":"Anomers (showing mutarotation to equilibrium ~+52.5°)","C":"Enantiomers","D":"Chain isomers"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 1, 'Isomers differing at cyclic hemiacetal anomeric C-1.', NULL, NULL, 'Isomers differing at cyclic hemiacetal anomeric C-1.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006f', 'Coagulation (curdling) of milk by lemon/heat and boiling of egg white illustrate:', '{"A":"Saponification","B":"Denaturation of proteins (secondary/tertiary H-bonds & 3D structure unfold while primary sequence remains intact)","C":"Zwitterion loss","D":"Glycosidic cleavage"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 1, 'Biological activity is lost while primary sequence stays intact.', NULL, NULL, 'Biological activity is lost while primary sequence stays intact.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000070', 'Water-soluble vitamins vs Fat-soluble vitamins respectively are:', '{"A":"Vitamins A, D, E, K vs B & C","B":"Vitamins B-group & C vs Vitamins A, D, E, K","C":"Vitamin C & D vs A & B₁₂","D":"Vitamin K only"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 1, 'B and C (except B₁₂ stored in liver) excrete in urine and need daily supply.', NULL, NULL, 'B and C (except B₁₂ stored in liver) excrete in urine and need daily supply.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000071', 'Which nitrogenous base is present in RNA but absent in DNA?', '{"A":"Adenine","B":"Uracil (replaces Thymine in RNA)","C":"Guanine","D":"Cytosine"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 1, 'DNA has A, G, C, T; RNA has A, G, C, U.', NULL, NULL, 'DNA has A, G, C, T; RNA has A, G, C, U.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000072', 'Assertion (A): Amylose is water-soluble (~15–20% of starch) whereas Amylopectin (~80–85%) is water-insoluble.
Reason (R): Amylose is a linear α-(1->4) polymer of α-D-glucose, whereas Amylopectin is a highly branched chain with α-(1->4) and α-(1->6) glycosidic linkages.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 1, 'Structural branching differentiates starch fractions.', NULL, NULL, 'Structural branching differentiates starch fractions.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000073', 'Give two structural reactions proving Glucose contains (i) a straight 6-carbon chain and (ii) five -OH groups.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 2, '(i) Prolonged heating with HI yields n-hexane. (ii) Acetylation with acetic anhydride forms glucose pentaacetate (5 -OH groups).', '1M each', NULL, '(i) Prolonged heating with HI yields n-hexane. (ii) Acetylation with acetic anhydride forms glucose pentaacetate (5 -OH groups).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000074', 'Distinguish between Fibrous proteins (Keratin/Myosin) and Globular proteins (Insulin/Albumin) with one property each.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 2, 'Fibrous: parallel polypeptide chains held by H/disulphide bonds, fibre-like, water-insoluble. Globular: chains coil spherically, water-soluble, functional metabolic roles.', '1M each', NULL, 'Fibrous: parallel polypeptide chains held by H/disulphide bonds, fibre-like, water-insoluble. Globular: chains coil spherically, water-soluble, functional metabolic roles.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000075', 'What is Peptide Linkage? Distinguish between Essential and Non-essential amino acids with two examples each.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 3, 'Amide -CO-NH- bond formed by condensation of -COOH of one amino acid with -NH₂ of another. Essential (must come in diet: Valine, Leucine, Lysine); Non-essential (synthesised by body: Glycine, Alanine).', 'Peptide bond (1M) + Distinction & examples (2M)', NULL, 'Amide -CO-NH- bond formed by condensation of -COOH of one amino acid with -NH₂ of another. Essential (must come in diet: Valine, Leucine, Lysine); Non-essential (synthesised by body: Glycine, Alanine).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000076', 'Name the vitamins whose deficiency causes: (i) Night blindness (Xerophthalmia), (ii) Scurvy (bleeding gums), (iii) Rickets / Osteomalacia, (iv) Beri-beri, (v) Pernicious anaemia.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 3, '(i) Vitamin A; (ii) Vitamin C (ascorbic acid); (iii) Vitamin D; (iv) Vitamin B₁ (Thiamine); (v) Vitamin B₁₂.', '3M (any 3–5 clearly matched)', NULL, '(i) Vitamin A; (ii) Vitamin C (ascorbic acid); (iii) Vitamin D; (iv) Vitamin B₁ (Thiamine); (v) Vitamin B₁₂.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000077', 'Explain why Glucose fails to react with Schiff''s reagent, NaHSO₃, and fails to form oxime from its pentaacetate — proving its cyclic hemiacetal Pyranose structure. Distinguish DNA vs RNA across sugar moiety, bases, strandedness, and cellular function. Explain Zwitterion behaviour of amino acids.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 5, 'Cyclic structure evidence (2M) + DNA vs RNA comparison (2M) + Zwitterion (1M).', 'Cyclic glucose proof (2M) + DNA vs RNA (2M) + Zwitterion (1M)', NULL, 'Cyclic structure evidence (2M) + DNA vs RNA comparison (2M) + Zwitterion (1M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000078', '(i) Hydrolysis products of Sucrose, Maltose, and Lactose. (ii) Why is hydrolysis of sucrose called "inversion of cane sugar"? (iii) Identify carbohydrate stored in animal liver/muscles ("animal starch"). (iv) Which linkages hold α-helix and β-pleated sheets together?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 4, '(i) Sucrose -> Glucose+Fructose; Maltose -> 2 Glucose; Lactose -> Galactose+Glucose. (ii) Dextrorotatory sucrose (+66.5°) yields equimolar mixture that is net laevorotatory (-19.9° due to fructose -92.4° > glucose +52.5°). (iii) Glycogen. (iv) Hydrogen bonds between C=O and N-H peptide backbones.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Food scientists test honey and syrup samples using polarimetry and Fehling''s reagent to distinguish inverting sucrose mixtures from non-reducing disaccharides.', '(i) Sucrose -> Glucose+Fructose; Maltose -> 2 Glucose; Lactose -> Galactose+Glucose. (ii) Dextrorotatory sucrose (+66.5°) yields equimolar mixture that is net laevorotatory (-19.9° due to fructose -92.4° > glucose +52.5°). (iii) Glycogen. (iv) Hydrogen bonds between C=O and N-H peptide backbones.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000079', 'A mixture of ethanol and acetone shows positive deviation from Raoult''s law because:', '{"A":"Ethanol-acetone hydrogen bonding is stronger than pure ethanol H-bonds","B":"Acetone molecules break existing hydrogen bonds between ethanol molecules, weakening intermolecular attraction","C":"Vapour pressure of mixture is lower than ideal","D":"Δ_mix H is negative"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Pure ethanol molecules are strongly H-bonded; adding acetone separates ethanol molecules and breaks some H-bonds, resulting in weaker A–B interactions, higher vapour pressure, Δ_mix H > 0 and Δ_mix V > 0.', 'Weaker A-B interactions -> positive deviation [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Positive deviation forms MINIMUM-boiling azeotropes; negative deviation (CHCl₃ + acetone, HNO₃ + H₂O) forms MAXIMUM-boiling azeotropes. | Common Pitfall: Confusing positive deviation with maximum-boiling azeotrope.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007a', 'Which of the following 0.1 M aqueous solutions will exhibit the HIGHEST boiling point (assume 100% dissociation)?', '{"A":"Glucose","B":"NaCl","C":"CaCl₂","D":"Al₂(SO₄)₃"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 1, 'Option (D) is correct. Elevation in boiling point ΔT_b = i·K_b·m. Al₂(SO₄)₃ dissociates into 2 Al³⁺ + 3 SO₄²⁻ -> van''t Hoff factor i = 5 (highest among glucose=1, NaCl=2, CaCl₂=3).', 'Al₂(SO₄)₃ gives i = 5 -> max ΔT_b [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: For highest freezing point (lowest ΔT_f), choose SMALLEST i (Glucose)! For highest boiling point / osmotic pressure, choose LARGEST i! | Common Pitfall: Selecting Glucose when highest boiling point is asked.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b', 'On dilution of both a strong electrolyte (KCl) and a weak electrolyte (CH₃COOH), which statement holds true?', '{"A":"Molar conductivity Λ_m increases for both, while conductivity κ decreases for both","B":"Both κ and Λ_m increase","C":"Λ_m decreases for weak electrolyte","D":"Conductivity κ increases on dilution"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Conductivity (κ) decreases on dilution because number of ions per unit volume decreases. Molar conductivity (Λ_m = κ × 1000 / M) increases due to rise in volume V (strong) and surge in degree of dissociation α (weak).', 'κ decreases (fewer ions/mL), Λ_m increases on dilution [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Memorize contrast: κ ALWAYS decreases with dilution; Λ_m ALWAYS increases with dilution. | Common Pitfall: Saying conductivity κ increases with dilution.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c', 'The rate constant of a chemical reaction has units mol⁻¹ L s⁻¹. What is the overall order of this reaction?', '{"A":"Zero order","B":"First order","C":"Second order","D":"Third order"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. General unit of k is (mol L⁻¹)^(1-n) s⁻¹. Setting 1 - n = -1 => n = 2 (Second order).', 'Apply (mol/L)^(1-n) s⁻¹ -> n = 2 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Zero order = mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹; First order = s⁻¹; Second order = mol⁻¹ L s⁻¹. | Common Pitfall: Mixing up zero-order and second-order signs.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d', 'Which of the following 3d transition metal ions has the highest magnetic moment (spin-only)?', '{"A":"Ti³⁺ (Z = 22)","B":"V²⁺ (Z = 23)","C":"Mn²⁺ (Z = 25)","D":"Cu²⁺ (Z = 29)"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'd- and f-Block Elements', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Mn²⁺ has [Ar] 3d⁵ configuration with 5 unpaired electrons, giving μ = √(5(5+2)) = √35 ≈ 5.92 BM (maximum among all 3d ions).', 'Mn²⁺ (3d⁵ -> 5 unpaired e⁻) => μ = 5.92 BM [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Recall μ = √(n(n+2)) BM; maximum unpaired electrons in d-orbitals is 5. | Common Pitfall: Confusing Mn²⁺ (3d⁵) with Fe²⁺ (3d⁶, only 4 unpaired e⁻).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e', 'The IUPAC name of the coordination complex [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl(NO₂)] is:', '{"A":"Diamminechloridonitrito-N-platinum(II)","B":"Diamminechloronitritoplatinum(IV)","C":"Platinum(II) diamminechloronitrite","D":"Nitrito-N-diamminechloroplatinum(0)"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Alphabetical ligand order: ammine, chlorido, nitrito-N (or nitro). Oxidation state of Pt: x + 0 - 1 - 1 = 0 => +2 (II). Name: Diamminechloridonitrito-N-platinum(II).', 'Alphabetical ligands + Pt(II) oxidation state [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Ignore "di/tri" prefixes when alphabetizing ligands (ammine -> chlorido -> nitrito). | Common Pitfall: Miscomputing Pt oxidation state as IV instead of II.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007f', 'Which complex ion is low-spin (inner orbital, d²sp³ hybridised) and diamagnetic?', '{"A":"[CoF₆]³⁻","B":"[FeF₆]³⁻","C":"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺","D":"[NiCl₄]²⁻"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Co³⁺ is 3d⁶. NH₃ is a strong-field ligand (Δ₀ > P), forcing all 6 electrons to pair in t₂g⁶ e_g⁰ (0 unpaired e⁻, diamagnetic, d²sp³). F⁻ is weak-field leaving [CoF₆]³⁻ high-spin paramagnetic (sp³d²).', 'Strong-field NH₃ pairs Co³⁺ 3d⁶ -> d²sp³ diamagnetic [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Strong-field ligands in NCERT: CO, CN⁻, en, NH₃ (for Co³⁺). | Common Pitfall: Thinking [CoF₆]³⁻ is diamagnetic.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000080', 'Which alkyl halide undergoes S_N2 nucleophilic substitution reaction at the FASTEST rate?', '{"A":"(CH₃)₃C–Br","B":"(CH₃)₂CH–Br","C":"CH₃CH₂–Br","D":"CH₃–Br"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes & Haloarenes', 1, 'Option (D) is correct. S_N2 proceeds via backside attack with a pentavalent transition state; least steric hindrance gives fastest rate: CH₃X > 1° > 2° > 3°.', 'Least steric hindrance -> CH₃–Br fastest in SN2 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: SN2 order: 0° (methyl) > 1° > 2° > 3°; SN1 order: 3° > 2° > 1°. | Common Pitfall: Selecting 3° butyl bromide (which is fastest for SN1, slowest for SN2).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000081', 'Lucas reagent (conc. HCl + anhydrous ZnCl₂) produces immediate turbidity at room temperature with:', '{"A":"Propan-1-ol","B":"Propan-2-ol","C":"2-Methylpropan-2-ol (tert-butyl alcohol)","D":"Phenol"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols & Ethers', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Lucas test proceeds via carbocation intermediate: 3° alcohols form very stable 3° carbocation and turn turbid IMMEDIATELY; 2° takes ~5 minutes; 1° shows no turbidity at room temp.', '3° alcohol (2-methylpropan-2-ol) -> immediate turbidity [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember 3° immediate, 2° in 5 min, 1° only on heating. | Common Pitfall: Choosing Phenol (which does not undergo C–O cleavage with Lucas).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000082', 'Which of the following aldehydes undergoes Cannizzaro reaction (self-oxidation-reduction) when heated with concentrated NaOH?', '{"A":"Ethanal (CH₃CHO)","B":"Propanal (CH₃CH₂CHO)","C":"Benzaldehyde (C₆H₅CHO)","D":"Butanal"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones & Carboxylic Acids', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Aldehydes lacking α-hydrogen atoms (like HCHO and C₆H₅CHO) undergo Cannizzaro disproportionation in conc. alkali to yield an alcohol and a carboxylate salt.', 'No α-hydrogen in Benzaldehyde -> Cannizzaro reaction [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: α-H present => Aldol condensation; No α-H (HCHO, C₆H₅CHO, (CH₃)₃CCHO) => Cannizzaro! | Common Pitfall: Choosing Ethanal (which has 3 α-hydrogens and gives Aldol).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000083', 'The correct decreasing order of basic strength of ethylamines in AQUEOUS solution is:', '{"A":"(C₂H₅)₃N > (C₂H₅)₂NH > C₂H₅NH₂ > NH₃","B":"(C₂H₅)₂NH > (C₂H₅)₃N > C₂H₅NH₂ > NH₃","C":"C₂H₅NH₂ > (C₂H₅)₂NH > (C₂H₅)₃N > NH₃","D":"NH₃ > C₂H₅NH₂ > (C₂H₅)₂NH > (C₂H₅)₃N"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Combining +I effect, solvation (hydration) stability, and steric hindrance in water: Ethyl series is 2° > 3° > 1° > NH₃ (whereas Methyl series is 2° > 1° > 3° > NH₃!).', 'Aqueous Ethyl order: (C₂H₅)₂NH > (C₂H₅)₃N > C₂H₅NH₂ > NH₃ [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Memorize NCERT aqueous code: Ethyl = 231, Methyl = 213! Gas phase is always 3 > 2 > 1. | Common Pitfall: Mixing up Methyl (213) and Ethyl (231) aqueous orders.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000084', 'Deficiency of Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) causes which disease, and which base is present in RNA but absent in DNA?', '{"A":"Beri-beri; Thymine","B":"Scurvy; Uracil","C":"Rickets; Cytosine","D":"Night blindness; Guanine"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Vitamin C deficiency causes Scurvy (bleeding gums). RNA contains Uracil (U) in place of Thymine (T) found in DNA.', 'Scurvy + Uracil in RNA [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Water-soluble vitamins: B-complex and C (cannot be stored long in body except B₁₂). | Common Pitfall: Swapping Uracil and Thymine.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000085', 'Assertion (A): Osmotic pressure method is preferred over boiling point elevation for determining molar masses of proteins and polymers.
Reason (R): Osmotic pressure is measured at room temperature and has appreciable magnitude even for very dilute solutions.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Biomolecules decompose on heating (unlike boiling point) and show tiny ΔT_b at low molarity, whereas π is large enough to read accurately at room temperature.', 'Option (A) citing room temp & measurable magnitude [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Also note molarity (M) is used in π = CRT instead of molality. | Common Pitfall: Saying polymers are insoluble in water.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000086', 'Assertion (A): Chlorobenzene is much less reactive towards nucleophilic substitution than chloroethane.
Reason (R): In chlorobenzene, the C–Cl bond acquires partial double-bond character due to resonance, and carbon is sp²-hybridised.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Haloarenes', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Resonance structures give C–Cl partial double-bond strength (shorter, harder to cleave), plus sp² C holds electrons tightly and phenyl cation is unstable.', 'Option (A) citing resonance partial double-bond & sp² carbon [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember all 4 NCERT reasons for haloarene inertness in S_N. | Common Pitfall: Marking (B).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000087', 'Assertion (A): Trichloroacetic acid (CCl₃COOH) is a stronger acid than acetic acid (CH₃COOH).
Reason (R): Three strongly electron-withdrawing -Cl atoms exert a strong -I effect, dispersing the negative charge of the conjugate carboxylate ion and stabilising it.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes & Ketones', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Electron-withdrawing groups (-I) stabilise RCOO⁻ and increase K_a (decrease pK_a), whereas +I groups like -CH₃ decrease acidity.', 'Option (A) citing -I stabilisation of conjugate base [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Higher K_a = Lower pK_a = Stronger Acid! | Common Pitfall: Confusing +I vs -I effects.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000088', 'Assertion (A): Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts alkylation or acylation.
Reason (R): Aniline acts as a Lewis base and reacts with the Lewis acid catalyst Anhydrous AlCl₃ to form a salt, turning nitrogen positively charged and strongly deactivating the ring.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. C₆H₅NH₂ + AlCl₃ → C₆H₅NH₂⁺ AlCl₃⁻; the positively charged N strongly withdraws electrons (-I), deactivating the benzene ring toward electrophilic substitution.', 'Option (A) explaining AlCl₃ salt formation & deactivation [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Same reason applies why nitration of aniline gives unexpected 47% meta-nitroaniline (anilinium ion formation in strong acid)! | Common Pitfall: Saying -NH₂ itself is a deactivating group (it is actually strongly activating when free!).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000089', 'How many Coulombs of electricity are required to reduce 1 mole of MnO₄⁻ to Mn²⁺ in acidic medium? Also write the overall reaction of an H₂–O₂ fuel cell.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 2, 'Reduction half: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O.
5 moles of electrons are required per mole MnO₄⁻ => Charge Q = 5 F = 5 × 96500 C = 482,500 C.
H₂–O₂ Fuel Cell overall reaction: 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l).', '5 F = 482,500 C calculation [1 Mark] | Fuel cell reaction 2H₂ + O₂ -> 2H₂O [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Oxidation state change of Mn: +7 -> +2 => n = 5 electrons. | Common Pitfall: Using n = 3 (MnO₂ neutral product) instead of n = 5.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008a', 'Show that for a first-order reaction, the time required for completion of 99.9% of the reaction is approximately 10 times its half-life (t₁/₂). (Given log 2 = 0.3010)', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 2, 't₁/₂ = 0.693 / k.
For 99.9% completion: [R]₀ = 100, remaining [R] = 100 - 99.9 = 0.1.
t_99.9% = (2.303 / k) log₁₀(100 / 0.1) = (2.303 / k) log₁₀(10³) = (2.303 × 3) / k = 6.909 / k.
Ratio t_99.9% / t₁/₂ = (6.909 / k) / (0.693 / k) = 6.909 / 0.693 ≈ 9.97 ≈ 10.', 'Compute t_99.9% = 6.909/k using remaining [R] = 0.1 [1 Mark] | Take ratio 6.909 / 0.693 ≈ 10 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember: Remaining concentration [R] = [R]₀ - 99.9% = 0.1% of [R]₀! | Common Pitfall: Plugging 99.9 in the denominator instead of remaining 0.1.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008b', 'What is Lanthanoid contraction? Mention two important consequences of lanthanoid contraction.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'd- and f-Block', 2, 'Lanthanoid contraction: Steady decrease in atomic and ionic radii from La³⁺ to Lu³⁺ across the 4f series due to poor shielding effect of inner 4f electrons against increasing nuclear charge.
Consequences:
1. Zr (4d) and Hf (5d) have almost identical atomic radii (160 pm vs 159 pm) and very similar chemical properties.
2. Basicity of lanthanoid hydroxides decreases steadily from La(OH)₃ to Lu(OH)₃.', 'Definition + poor 4f shielding [1 Mark] | Two consequences (Zr/Hf pair & hydroxide basicity) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Poor shielding order: s > p > d > f (4f shields very poorly!). | Common Pitfall: Saying nuclear charge decreases across lanthanoids.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008c', 'Write the balanced chemical equations for:
(i) Reimer-Tiemann reaction of phenol
(ii) Williamson synthesis of tert-butyl ethyl ether (CH₃)₃C–O–C₂H₅ using proper reactants.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols & Ethers', 2, '(i) Reimer-Tiemann: Phenol + CHCl₃ + 3NaOH --(340K)--> Sodium salicylate intermediate --(H⁺)--> Salicylaldehyde (2-hydroxybenzaldehyde) + 3NaCl + 2H₂O.
(ii) Williamson Synthesis: Must use 3° alkoxide + 1° alkyl halide (NOT 3° halide, which gives alkene elimination!):
(CH₃)₃C–O⁻Na⁺ + CH₃CH₂–Br → (CH₃)₃C–O–C₂H₅ + NaBr.', '(i) Phenol + CHCl₃ + aq. NaOH -> Salicylaldehyde [1 Mark] | (ii) Sodium tert-butoxide + ethyl bromide -> ether [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: In Williamson synthesis of tert-butyl ethers, NEVER choose (CH₃)₃C-Br + C₂H₅ONa — it produces 2-methylpropene instead of ether! | Common Pitfall: Using tert-butyl bromide in Williamson synthesis.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008d', 'What is meant by denaturation of a protein? Give one example, and state which levels of protein structure are destroyed vs retained.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Chemistry', 'Biomolecules', 2, 'Denaturation: Disruption of native 3D biological structure of a protein due to physical change (heat) or chemical change (pH alteration), disrupting hydrogen bonds so globules unfold and protein loses biological activity.
Example: Coagulation of egg white on boiling, or curdling of milk.
Effect on structure: Secondary and Tertiary structures are destroyed, while the Primary structure (peptide sequence) remains intact.', 'Definition + example (boiled egg white / curdling) [1 Mark] | 2° & 3° destroyed; 1° structure remains intact [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Primary peptide bonds are NOT broken during denaturation. | Common Pitfall: Saying primary amino acid sequence is broken.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008e', 'Calculate the freezing point of a solution containing 60 g of glucose (Molar mass = 180 g/mol) in 250 g of water (K_f for water = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹). How would the result change if 0.33 mol of NaCl were used instead?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Solutions', 3, 'Moles of glucose = 60 / 180 = 1/3 mol.
Mass of solvent = 250 g = 0.25 kg.
Molality m = (1/3) / 0.25 = 4/3 ≈ 1.333 mol/kg.
ΔT_f = K_f · m = 1.86 × (4/3) = 2.48 K.
Freezing point = 273.15 - 2.48 = 270.67 K (or -2.48°C).
With 0.33 mol NaCl (i ≈ 2), moles of ions double => ΔT_f ≈ 4.96 K -> freezing point drops twice as much (-4.96°C).', 'Molality m = 1.333 mol/kg [1 Mark] | ΔT_f = 2.48 K -> T_f = -2.48°C (270.67 K) [1 Mark] | NaCl i=2 doubles depression [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Never forget to convert solvent mass from grams to kg in molality! | Common Pitfall: Taking 2.48°C instead of -2.48°C as final freezing point!', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008f', 'Calculate the EMF of the cell at 298 K:
Mg(s) | Mg²⁺ (0.001 M) || Cu²⁺ (0.0001 M) | Cu(s)
Given: E°(Mg²⁺/Mg) = -2.36 V, E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V. (Use 0.059/2 ≈ 0.0295)', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 3, 'Cell reaction: Mg(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Mg²⁺(aq) + Cu(s) (n = 2 electrons).
E°_cell = E°_cathode - E°_anode = 0.34 - (-2.36) = +2.70 V.
Nernst Equation at 298 K:
E_cell = E°_cell - (0.059 / 2) log₁₀([Mg²⁺] / [Cu²⁺])
= 2.70 - 0.0295 log₁₀(10⁻³ / 10⁻⁴) = 2.70 - 0.0295 log₁₀(10)
= 2.70 - 0.0295 = 2.6705 V.', 'E°_cell = 2.70 V & n = 2 [1 Mark] | Nernst setup [Mg²⁺]/[Cu²⁺] = 10 [1 Mark] | E_cell = 2.67 V [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Put Anode product concentration [Mg²⁺] in numerator and Cathode reactant [Cu²⁺] in denominator! | Common Pitfall: Inverting ratio as [Cu²⁺]/[Mg²⁺] or taking n = 1.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000090', 'The rate of a reaction doubles when the temperature increases from 298 K to 308 K. Calculate the activation energy E_a of the reaction. (R = 8.314 J/mol·K, log 2 = 0.3010)', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Chemical Kinetics', 3, 'Using Arrhenius two-temperature equation:
log₁₀(k₂ / k₁) = [E_a / (2.303 R)] × [ (T₂ - T₁) / (T₁ T₂) ]
log₁₀(2) = [E_a / (2.303 × 8.314)] × [ 10 / (298 × 308) ]
0.3010 = (E_a / 19.147) × (10 / 91784)
E_a = (0.3010 × 19.147 × 91784) / 10 ≈ 52,898 J/mol = 52.9 kJ/mol.', 'Arrhenius formula with k₂/k₁ = 2 [1 Mark] | Substitute T₁=298 K, T₂=308 K [1 Mark] | E_a ≈ 52.9 kJ/mol [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Write E_a in both J/mol and kJ/mol cleanly. | Common Pitfall: Using Celsius 25 and 35 instead of Kelvin 298 and 308.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000091', 'Using Valence Bond Theory, explain why [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is square planar and diamagnetic whereas [NiCl₄]²⁻ is tetrahedral and paramagnetic (Atomic number of Ni = 28).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Coordination Compounds', 3, 'Ni²⁺ has 3d⁸ configuration (two unpaired e⁻ in 3d).
1. In [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻, CN⁻ is a strong-field ligand causing pairing of the two unpaired 3d electrons, vacating one 3d orbital. Hybridisation is dsp² -> Square Planar geometry and diamagnetic (0 unpaired e⁻).
2. In [NiCl₄]²⁻, Cl⁻ is a weak-field ligand unable to pair 3d electrons -> outer 4s and 4p orbitals hybridise (sp³) -> Tetrahedral geometry with 2 unpaired electrons (paramagnetic, μ ≈ 2.83 BM).', 'Ni²⁺ 3d⁸ ground state [0.5 Mark] | [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ pairing -> dsp², square planar, diamagnetic [1.25 Marks] | [NiCl₄]²⁻ no pairing -> sp³, tetrahedral, paramagnetic (2 unpaired e⁻) [1.25 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Both have coordination number 4, making it the classic CN=4 VBT comparison! | Common Pitfall: Assigning sp³d² instead of dsp² / sp³.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000092', '(a) Explain why haloalkanes react with KCN to form alkyl cyanides as major products, whereas with AgCN they yield isocyanides.
(b) Convert Propene to Propan-1-ol.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Haloalkanes', 3, '(a) CN⁻ is an ambident nucleophile (can attack through C or N). KCN is predominantly ionic, leaving C-end free to form stronger C–C bond (alkyl cyanide R–CN). AgCN is largely covalent, so the lone pair on Nitrogen attacks R–X forming isocyanide (R–NC).
(b) Propene + HBr (with peroxide) → 1-Bromopropane --(aq. KOH)--> Propan-1-ol (or Hydroboration-Oxidation directly with B₂H₆ followed by H₂O₂/OH⁻).', '(a) Ionic KCN (R-CN) vs covalent AgCN (R-NC) ambident logic [2 Marks] | (b) Hydroboration-oxidation or peroxide-HBr + aq. KOH [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Same ambident distinction applies to KNO₂ (R-ONO) vs AgNO₂ (R-NO₂)! | Common Pitfall: Saying KCN is covalent and AgCN is ionic.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000093', '(a) Why cannot aromatic primary amines (like Aniline) be prepared by Gabriel phthalimide synthesis?
(b) How does Hinsberg''s reagent (benzenesulphonyl chloride, C₆H₅SO₂Cl) distinguish between primary, secondary, and tertiary amines?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Amines', 3, '(a) Gabriel phthalimide synthesis involves S_N2 nucleophilic attack of phthalimide anion on an alkyl halide. Aryl halides (like chlorobenzene) do NOT undergo nucleophilic substitution easily due to partial double-bond character of C–X bond, so aniline cannot be prepared.
(b) With C₆H₅SO₂Cl:
• 1° Amine forms N-alkylbenzenesulphonamide which dissolves in aq. KOH (has acidic N–H proton).
• 2° Amine forms N,N-dialkylbenzenesulphonamide which is INSOLUBLE in aq. KOH (no acidic N–H).
• 3° Amine does NOT react at all.', '(a) Aryl halides resist SN2 attack by phthalimide ion [1 Mark] | (b) Hinsberg observations for 1°, 2°, 3° amines [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Note: Hinsberg reagent distinguishes amines by presence of acidic H on sulphonamide nitrogen! | Common Pitfall: Thinking 2° amine product dissolves in KOH.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000094', 'Arrange the following in increasing order of acidic strength and justify: Propan-1-ol, Phenol, 4-Nitrophenol, 4-Methylphenol (p-cresol).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols, Phenols & Ethers', 3, 'Increasing acidic strength: Propan-1-ol < 4-Methylphenol < Phenol < 4-Nitrophenol.
Justification:
• Phenols are vastly more acidic than aliphatic alcohols because phenoxide ion is resonance-stabilised.
• In 4-nitrophenol, the strongly electron-withdrawing -NO₂ group (-R and -I) disperses negative charge on phenoxide oxygen, increasing acidity.
• In 4-methylphenol, electron-donating -CH₃ (+I and hyperconjugation) intensifies negative charge, destabilising phenoxide ion and decreasing acidity.', 'Correct sequence Propan-1-ol < 4-Methylphenol < Phenol < 4-Nitrophenol [1 Mark] | Resonance stabilisation of phenoxide + -NO₂ vs -CH₃ effects [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember: EWG (-NO₂) increases phenol acidity; EDG (-CH₃, -OCH₃) decreases phenol acidity. | Common Pitfall: Placing 4-methylphenol above phenol.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000095', 'Read the case and answer:
For a first-order decomposition reaction R → Products, the concentration of reactant drops from 0.8 M to 0.2 M in 40 minutes at 300 K. (Given log 2 = 0.3010)

(a) Determine the half-life (t₁/₂) of this reaction. (1M)
(b) Calculate the first-order rate constant k in min⁻¹. (1.5M)
(c) What fraction of initial reactant will remain after 60 minutes from the start? (1.5M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Concentration dropped from 0.8 M -> 0.4 M -> 0.2 M (2 half-lives in 40 minutes) => t₁/₂ = 40 / 2 = 20 minutes.
(b) k = 0.693 / t₁/₂ = 0.693 / 20 = 0.03465 min⁻¹.
(c) 60 minutes = 3 half-lives (60/20 = 3) => Remaining fraction = (1/2)³ = 1/8 = 0.125 (12.5%).', '(a) 2 half-lives = 40 min -> t₁/₂ = 20 min [1 Mark] | (b) k = 0.03465 min⁻¹ [1.5 Marks] | (c) (1/2)³ = 1/8 = 12.5% [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: First-order half-life is strictly INDEPENDENT of initial concentration! | Common Pitfall: Using zero-order t₁/₂ formula [R]₀/(2k).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000096', 'Read the case and answer:
Glucose is an aldohexose that exists primarily in two cyclic hemiacetal forms (α-D-glucose and β-D-glucose) differing only in configuration around C-1 carbon. Despite having an aldehyde group, glucose does not give Schiff''s test or react with NaHSO₃.

(a) What name is given to the cyclic pair α-D-glucose and β-D-glucose? (1M)
(b) Name the reaction and product when glucose is treated with (i) Bromine water, and (ii) Conc. HNO₃. (2M)
(c) Why are sucrose and starch classified differently with respect to Fehling''s reducing test? (1M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Chemistry', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Anomers (differing only at C-1 anomeric carbon).
(b) (i) Bromine water (mild oxidant) oxidises -CHO group only -> Gluconic acid.
(ii) Conc. HNO₃ oxidises both -CHO and primary -CH₂OH -> Saccharic acid (dicarboxylic acid).
(c) Sucrose has both glucose C-1 and fructose C-2 anomeric carbons locked in glycosidic linkage -> NON-reducing sugar (no free hemiacetal), whereas maltose/lactose are reducing sugars.', '(a) Anomers [1 Mark] | (b) Gluconic acid & Saccharic acid [2 Marks] | (c) Glycosidic bond locks reducing groups in sucrose [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember: Br₂ water -> Gluconic acid; Conc. HNO₃ -> Saccharic acid; HI + Δ -> n-Hexane. | Common Pitfall: Confusing Gluconic acid with Saccharic acid.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000097', '(a) Write balanced ionic equations for the oxidising action of acidified potassium permanganate (MnO₄⁻) on:
  (i) Oxalate ion (C₂O₄²⁻)
  (ii) Ferrous ion (Fe²⁺)
  (iii) Iodide ion (I⁻) in neutral/faintly alkaline medium.
(b) Account for the following:
  (i) Transition metals form a large number of complex compounds and act as good catalysts.
  (ii) Cu⁺ ion is unstable in aqueous solution and disproportionates.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'd- and f-Block Elements', 5, '(a) (i) 2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O.
(ii) MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ + 8H⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O.
(iii) 2MnO₄⁻ + H₂O + I⁻ → 2MnO₂ + 2OH⁻ + IO₃⁻ (iodate!).
(b) (i) Complexes: small ionic size, high ionic charge, availability of vacant d-orbitals. Catalysis: ability to adopt variable oxidation states and provide surface area for adsorption.
(ii) 2Cu⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + Cu(s). Although Cu⁺ has stable 3d¹⁰, the much higher negative hydration enthalpy (Δ_hyd H) of tiny doubly-charged Cu²⁺ more than compensates for the high second ionisation enthalpy of Cu.', '(a) All 3 balanced redox ionic equations (note IO₃⁻ in neutral/alkaline!) [3 Marks] | (b) Complex/catalyst reasons + high hydration enthalpy of Cu²⁺ [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Watch out: MnO₄⁻ oxidises I⁻ to I₂ in ACIDIC medium, but to IO₃⁻ in NEUTRAL/FAINTLY ALKALINE medium! | Common Pitfall: Writing I₂ instead of IO₃⁻ in neutral/alkaline medium.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000098', '(a) Write chemical equations for:
  (i) Cross-Aldol or Self-Aldol condensation of Ethanal
  (ii) HVZ (Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky) reaction of Propanoic acid
  (iii) Rosenmund reduction of Benzoyl chloride.
(b) Give simple chemical tests to distinguish between:
  (i) Propanal and Propanone
  (ii) Phenol and Benzoic acid.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, Ketones & Carboxylic Acids', 5, '(a) (i) 2 CH₃CHO --(dil. NaOH)--> CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CHO (3-Hydroxybutanal) --(Heat)--> CH₃CH=CHCHO (But-2-enal) + H₂O.
(ii) CH₃CH₂COOH --(Cl₂/Red P, H₂O)--> CH₃CH(Cl)COOH (2-Chloropropanoic acid).
(iii) C₆H₅COCl + H₂ --(Pd-BaSO₄)--> C₆H₅CHO + HCl.
(b) (i) Propanal gives silver mirror with Tollens'' reagent (or red Cu₂O ppt with Fehling''s); Propanone does NOT (conversely, Propanone gives yellow iodoform ppt with I₂/NaOH).
(ii) Benzoic acid gives brisk effervescence of CO₂ with NaHCO₃ solution; Phenol does not (or Phenol gives violet colour with neutral FeCl₃).', '(a) Aldol, HVZ & Rosenmund equations [3 Marks] | (b) Tollens/Fehling & NaHCO₃/FeCl₃ tests [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Both Tollens'' and Fehling''s distinguish aldehydes from ketones (Note: aromatic aldehydes like Benzaldehyde do NOT respond to Fehling''s test!). | Common Pitfall: Using Fehling''s test to detect Benzaldehyde.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12021111-0000-4000-8000-000000000099', '(a) State Kohlrausch''s law of independent migration of ions. Calculate Λ°_m for acetic acid (CH₃COOH) given:
  Λ°_m(HCl) = 425.9 S cm² mol⁻¹, Λ°_m(NaCl) = 126.4 S cm² mol⁻¹, Λ°_m(CH₃COONa) = 91.0 S cm² mol⁻¹.
(b) If conductivity of 0.00241 M acetic acid is 7.896 × 10⁻⁵ S cm⁻¹, calculate its molar conductivity Λ_m and degree of dissociation α (using Λ°_m = 390.5 S cm² mol⁻¹).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Chemistry', 'Electrochemistry', 5, '(a) Kohlrausch Law: Limiting molar conductivity of an electrolyte equals the sum of individual limiting ionic conductivities of its cation and anion.
Λ°_m(CH₃COOH) = Λ°_m(CH₃COONa) + Λ°_m(HCl) - Λ°_m(NaCl) = 91.0 + 425.9 - 126.4 = 390.5 S cm² mol⁻¹.
(b) Λ_m = (κ × 1000) / M = (7.896 × 10⁻⁵ × 1000) / 0.00241 = 0.07896 / 0.00241 = 32.76 S cm² mol⁻¹.
Degree of dissociation α = Λ_m / Λ°_m = 32.76 / 390.5 ≈ 0.084 (8.4%).', '(a) Statement + 390.5 S cm² mol⁻¹ calculation [2.5 Marks] | (b) Λ_m = 32.76 & α = 0.084 [2.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Check units carefully: κ in S/cm uses factor 1000 in (κ × 1000)/M. | Common Pitfall: Subtracting NaCl twice or omitting factor 1000.', 'HARD', true)
